1
00:01:03,043 --> 00:01:06,882
What would happen if the Orcs
captured Frodo and got the ring?

2
00:01:06,883 --> 00:01:09,162
- Felix.
- They'd have done bad things with it.

3
00:01:09,163 --> 00:01:10,962
And what sort of bad things
would they have done?

4
00:01:10,963 --> 00:01:13,882
- They'd have killed Gandalf.
- They'd have thrown him in the fire.

5
00:01:13,883 --> 00:01:16,562
He can survive fire. Fire can't get him.

6
00:01:16,563 --> 00:01:20,042
Sir, sir. They'd have captured the world
and made everyone their slave.

7
00:01:20,043 --> 00:01:22,202
Now, would it be a good thing
to be a slave of the Orcs?

8
00:01:22,203 --> 00:01:25,002
- No.
- why is being made a slave a bad thing?

9
00:01:25,003 --> 00:01:27,722
Because you don't get no money
and slaves have to work all the time

10
00:01:27,723 --> 00:01:29,202
and don't get no holidays.

11
00:01:29,203 --> 00:01:31,842
That's right. Now, what happens if you
have to work all the time

12
00:01:31,843 --> 00:01:34,242
and don't get any holidays? Molly.

13
00:01:34,243 --> 00:01:36,242
- You get tired out.
- You certainly do.

14
00:01:36,243 --> 00:01:38,722
It means you don't have any freedom.

15
00:01:38,723 --> 00:01:41,443
Now, what is freedom? Freya.

16
00:01:41,523 --> 00:01:43,362
When you can go out to shops
to buy what you want

17
00:01:43,363 --> 00:01:44,762
and go out to play when you want.

18
00:01:44,763 --> 00:01:47,922
That's right. Freedom is when
we can decide what to do or buy,

19
00:01:47,923 --> 00:01:49,122
where to go and when.

20
00:01:49,123 --> 00:01:52,322
Freedom is the right
to choose for yourself. Molly.

21
00:01:52,323 --> 00:01:54,042
Are there slaves now?

22
00:01:54,043 --> 00:01:56,362
Well, there are different
sorts of slaves in the world.

23
00:01:56,363 --> 00:02:01,454
You can be a slave to television,
or to work, or to food.

24
00:02:01,883 --> 00:02:03,611
Are they Orcs, sir?

25
00:02:03,683 --> 00:02:06,562
Well, they wouldn't like
to be called that, Felix.

26
00:02:06,563 --> 00:02:09,282
But they can take away your freedom.

27
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:13,082
You be looking at your worksheets,
questions one to five,

28
00:02:13,083 --> 00:02:17,720
and be going on with the section
about Roman slaves.

29
00:02:36,083 --> 00:02:37,123
Cuffs on.

30
00:02:37,443 --> 00:02:39,443
Come on. Your head up.

31
00:02:53,163 --> 00:02:56,618
Sir Ian, we're about to see Sir Monty.

32
00:02:57,003 --> 00:02:58,043
Mmm.

33
00:02:58,563 --> 00:03:04,122
We'd prefer not to have to remind him
about the criminal court reporter, sir.

34
00:03:04,123 --> 00:03:05,163
Yes.

35
00:03:06,003 --> 00:03:08,762
Of course, sir. If I have to, I will.

36
00:03:08,763 --> 00:03:09,854
Ok, bye-bye.

37
00:03:11,563 --> 00:03:13,762
You can tell Sir Ian that I appreciate

38
00:03:13,763 --> 00:03:15,842
the court reporter being posted
to Cardiff,

39
00:03:15,843 --> 00:03:19,362
but I don't want Deed on my circuit.
Not again.

40
00:03:19,363 --> 00:03:22,242
But we thought you and Mr Justice Deed
were reconciled, sir.

41
00:03:22,243 --> 00:03:24,682
Oh, there's a rapprochement.
we are brethren, after all.

42
00:03:24,683 --> 00:03:26,002
But that's as far as it goes.

43
00:03:26,003 --> 00:03:28,162
Unfortunately, neither the presider
on the northern,

44
00:03:28,163 --> 00:03:29,602
nor the one on the western circuit

45
00:03:29,603 --> 00:03:31,322
wants Sir John Deed sitting there.

46
00:03:31,323 --> 00:03:33,802
Well, that's hardly my problem,
Mr James.

47
00:03:33,803 --> 00:03:37,842
Mr Justice Deed has many
fine qualities, Sir Monty.

48
00:03:37,843 --> 00:03:41,202
Oh, then I'm surprised you're having
such difficulties placing him.

49
00:03:41,203 --> 00:03:43,602
Are all the presiders resisting him?

50
00:03:43,603 --> 00:03:46,322
Sir Ian thought he'd be best off
back in Sussex

51
00:03:46,323 --> 00:03:49,682
because of the problems with one
of the circuit judges there.

52
00:03:49,683 --> 00:03:51,047
Judge Bromford.

53
00:03:51,723 --> 00:03:55,282
He's had 37 judgements
appealed in the last two years.

54
00:03:55,283 --> 00:03:58,283
- His cases are piling up.
- Yes.

55
00:03:58,763 --> 00:04:02,922
Yes, I suppose we could have
the lower judge's cases

56
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:04,196
heard by Deed.

57
00:04:04,483 --> 00:04:05,882
That will put him in his place.

58
00:04:05,883 --> 00:04:09,322
He could hardly get into trouble
with such uncontroversial cases,

59
00:04:09,323 --> 00:04:10,323
Sir Monty.

60
00:04:18,563 --> 00:04:19,722
(DOOR OPENING)

61
00:04:19,723 --> 00:04:22,162
Look who I found skulking outside.

62
00:04:22,163 --> 00:04:25,709
I wasn't skulking, Vera. I never skulk.

63
00:04:26,843 --> 00:04:30,025
John. You're coming back to Sussex.

64
00:04:30,163 --> 00:04:32,842
Well, I daresay neither of us
is pleased about that, Monty.

65
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:35,362
No, perhaps we should try to
put the past behind us.

66
00:04:35,363 --> 00:04:36,682
Is that possible?

67
00:04:36,683 --> 00:04:39,162
I've just been looking through the list.

68
00:04:39,163 --> 00:04:42,442
I can't see anything that could be
seriously contentious.

69
00:04:42,443 --> 00:04:45,202
I don't exactly court contention,
you know.

70
00:04:45,203 --> 00:04:47,842
It seems to find you nonetheless.

71
00:04:47,843 --> 00:04:50,962
You know, there are a number
of burglaries you could hear.

72
00:04:50,963 --> 00:04:53,362
Possession of class-A drugs.

73
00:04:53,363 --> 00:04:56,962
Domestic violence.
There are a number of those.

74
00:04:56,963 --> 00:04:59,162
A purple judge
could hear those equally well.

75
00:04:59,163 --> 00:05:02,163
Yes, but they're coming your way.

76
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:05,322
What are you hearing?

77
00:05:05,323 --> 00:05:09,283
Oh, attempted murder and robbery.

78
00:05:09,443 --> 00:05:12,682
Man's been on the run for years.
Police have only just caught him.

79
00:05:12,683 --> 00:05:15,322
What about the schoolboy
who raped his teacher?

80
00:05:15,323 --> 00:05:18,762
- Well...
- Oh, Monty. we're hearing the rape.

81
00:05:18,763 --> 00:05:22,153
Yes. No, it's not ready yet, John.

82
00:05:22,243 --> 00:05:26,042
Well, I have a judicial review
to get out of the way first.

83
00:05:26,043 --> 00:05:28,482
Unless Vera wants to hear it.

84
00:05:28,483 --> 00:05:29,483
(LAUGHS)

85
00:05:46,643 --> 00:05:48,562
Anything improved with the list?

86
00:05:48,563 --> 00:05:51,642
Not unless you think embezzlement's
an improvement, Judge.

87
00:05:51,643 --> 00:05:52,683
I don't.

88
00:05:54,003 --> 00:05:56,882
See what you can come up with, Coop.

89
00:05:56,883 --> 00:05:59,520
Or I might go back to London.

90
00:06:01,723 --> 00:06:06,723
- Mrs Mills return my call yet?
- No, not on this phone.

91
00:06:12,763 --> 00:06:17,242
Hello, this is Jo Mills. Please leave
a message and I'll call you back.

92
00:06:17,243 --> 00:06:21,516
Jo, this is John. I'm back in Sussex,
and, um...

93
00:06:22,923 --> 00:06:26,322
It seems rather lonely
without Charlie down here.

94
00:06:26,323 --> 00:06:29,687
Um, wondering how the appeal's going.

95
00:06:30,363 --> 00:06:32,563
I'll talk to you later.

96
00:06:39,643 --> 00:06:42,002
Denny, I think it went well
for us in there.

97
00:06:42,003 --> 00:06:44,882
I didn't hear them say, "The
conviction's unsafe, Mr McDowell.

98
00:06:44,883 --> 00:06:46,762
- "You're free to go."
- Yet.

99
00:06:46,763 --> 00:06:51,582
Denny, they now know
how corrupt those policemen were.

100
00:06:57,323 --> 00:07:00,482
Mrs Mills? I'm Kerry Ramsay.
I'm a solicitor.

101
00:07:00,483 --> 00:07:02,682
Yes. I won't be taking on
Terry Rogers' defence.

102
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:04,562
I think your clerk
was hoping to persuade you.

103
00:07:04,563 --> 00:07:06,002
I told him I'm thinking
of taking a sabbatical.

104
00:07:06,003 --> 00:07:08,482
Well, yeah, he told me,
to spend some time with

105
00:07:08,483 --> 00:07:09,762
your adopted son.

106
00:07:09,763 --> 00:07:10,922
It hasn't got that far yet.

107
00:07:10,923 --> 00:07:13,202
Well, would you at least
read the papers?

108
00:07:13,203 --> 00:07:16,042
There hardly seems any point,
as I won't be taking it on.

109
00:07:16,043 --> 00:07:17,682
The case involves two detectives

110
00:07:17,683 --> 00:07:19,722
at the heart of the case
you're currently appealing,

111
00:07:19,723 --> 00:07:21,882
Matthew Stone and Troy Romano.

112
00:07:21,883 --> 00:07:24,202
It can't. They're retired.
Romano went to prison.

113
00:07:24,203 --> 00:07:27,202
Yes. Terry Rogers was arrested
16 years ago.

114
00:07:27,203 --> 00:07:31,642
He escaped. He'd been on the run
until his arrest at school.

115
00:07:31,643 --> 00:07:33,189
I'll have a look.

116
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:34,403
Okay.

117
00:07:57,203 --> 00:08:00,802
Michael was a bit upset earlier.
He thought you weren't coming.

118
00:08:00,803 --> 00:08:03,482
I'm sorry. Something unexpected came up.

119
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:05,842
He's having difficulty settling down.

120
00:08:05,843 --> 00:08:07,962
We thought the foster mother
he went to would settle him.

121
00:08:07,963 --> 00:08:10,922
Yeah. I blame myself. I shouldn't
have tried walking away.

122
00:08:10,923 --> 00:08:13,882
He'll be troublesome
if you do take him on.

123
00:08:13,883 --> 00:08:15,323
I'll get him.

124
00:08:27,963 --> 00:08:30,922
Hello, Michael. Sorry I'm late.
I had some urgent work.

125
00:08:30,923 --> 00:08:33,682
I always take on far too much.
Bet you don't.

126
00:08:33,683 --> 00:08:35,322
I'll bet you do everything
that you plan to do.

127
00:08:35,323 --> 00:08:37,442
Adults are so stupid. we always try...

128
00:08:37,443 --> 00:08:38,807
It's all right.

129
00:08:40,363 --> 00:08:42,242
Would you like to go out for some tea?

130
00:08:42,243 --> 00:08:44,122
We could go out.
Your house mother said that we...

131
00:08:44,123 --> 00:08:47,760
Look, she's not my mother.
My mum's dead.

132
00:08:50,123 --> 00:08:52,282
No, it's all right, it's all right,
it's all right.

133
00:08:52,283 --> 00:08:56,465
It's all right.
It's all right. It's all right.

134
00:09:11,683 --> 00:09:14,295
If you take Michael on,
it won't be easy.

135
00:09:14,296 --> 00:09:16,775
He's got
some serious emotional problems.

136
00:09:16,776 --> 00:09:20,095
Yes. The biggest of them seems
to be his not having a mother.

137
00:09:20,096 --> 00:09:24,655
Mrs Tredaway, can you even imagine what
that must be like for an 8-year-old?

138
00:09:24,656 --> 00:09:26,929
I lost my mother at nine.

139
00:09:28,576 --> 00:09:29,576
I'm sorry.

140
00:09:31,016 --> 00:09:33,735
- Did you have a father?
- Yeah.

141
00:09:33,736 --> 00:09:37,415
Yeah, that was some
compensation, of course.

142
00:09:37,416 --> 00:09:39,655
Sometimes people come in and think
all they have to do

143
00:09:39,656 --> 00:09:41,055
is offer the child love.

144
00:09:41,056 --> 00:09:44,975
It is a big move in
the right direction, Mrs Tredaway.

145
00:09:44,976 --> 00:09:48,975
Michael was put with
two very nice foster parents.

146
00:09:48,976 --> 00:09:50,775
What if he gets equally upset with you

147
00:09:50,776 --> 00:09:54,375
and starts to smash up your lovely home
or starts to steal your things?

148
00:09:54,376 --> 00:09:57,295
You talk as though you don't want
people adopting children.

149
00:09:57,296 --> 00:10:01,024
You didn't answer the question,
Mrs Mills.

150
00:10:02,776 --> 00:10:06,215
- Perhaps they didn't handle him right.
- No.

151
00:10:06,216 --> 00:10:08,853
No, they're very experienced.

152
00:10:08,856 --> 00:10:12,175
What we'd prefer not to have happen
is you coming back two week later

153
00:10:12,176 --> 00:10:13,775
to say you can't cope.

154
00:10:13,776 --> 00:10:16,867
Well, I might give it three weeks.

155
00:10:17,136 --> 00:10:18,136
Hmm.

156
00:10:19,896 --> 00:10:23,615
I did bring up two sons
without their father.

157
00:10:23,616 --> 00:10:26,215
Well, he will need a lot of input.

158
00:10:26,216 --> 00:10:28,135
How's that gonna stack up
with your career?

159
00:10:28,136 --> 00:10:30,975
I'm prepared to give up work
to take care of Michael.

160
00:10:30,976 --> 00:10:33,455
- Is that gonna be a problem?
- what, financially?

161
00:10:33,456 --> 00:10:36,296
No. I can still write articles,

162
00:10:36,376 --> 00:10:37,815
be a consultant.

163
00:10:37,816 --> 00:10:39,655
Well, sometimes stepping off
the career ladder

164
00:10:39,656 --> 00:10:41,055
can cause enormous resentment.

165
00:10:41,056 --> 00:10:43,420
I have thought about this.

166
00:10:44,856 --> 00:10:50,295
Michael is obviously suffering and
I believe that I can help change that.

167
00:10:50,296 --> 00:10:52,535
Well, what I have to advise
the selection committee on

168
00:10:52,536 --> 00:10:54,375
and what I want to be quite sure of

169
00:10:54,376 --> 00:10:56,895
is your reason for wanting to adopt.

170
00:10:56,896 --> 00:10:59,695
Especially as you're on your own.

171
00:10:59,696 --> 00:11:02,295
We have to be certain you're not
trying to replace something missing

172
00:11:02,296 --> 00:11:04,416
from your own life

173
00:11:04,496 --> 00:11:08,587
such as your children
going off to university.

174
00:11:17,176 --> 00:11:18,576
(DOORBELL)

175
00:11:25,296 --> 00:11:26,366
Oh, gosh.

176
00:11:26,656 --> 00:11:27,656
You okay?

177
00:11:27,776 --> 00:11:31,975
Need to talk to you
about the Denny McDowell appeal.

178
00:11:31,976 --> 00:11:34,815
I've only left you about 93 messages.

179
00:11:34,816 --> 00:11:36,135
Do you want some food? It's takeaway.

180
00:11:36,136 --> 00:11:37,255
If you've got some to spare.

181
00:11:37,256 --> 00:11:40,893
Yeah, sure. Go in. I'll get you a plate.

182
00:11:41,736 --> 00:11:44,695
I'm not convinced
I'm making our case for Denny.

183
00:11:44,696 --> 00:11:47,215
If you're not convinced, how are you
gonna convince the Appeal Court?

184
00:11:47,216 --> 00:11:49,095
You could give me a little
encouragement here.

185
00:11:49,096 --> 00:11:50,096
I did.

186
00:11:50,176 --> 00:11:52,255
I told you I thought the man
was a liar and a thief,

187
00:11:52,256 --> 00:11:57,215
but that I thought he was innocent
of this particular crime.

188
00:11:57,216 --> 00:11:59,126
What are you arguing?

189
00:11:59,976 --> 00:12:03,975
That the conviction is unsafe
both because of the DNA reprofiling,

190
00:12:03,976 --> 00:12:06,455
the known corrupt record
of two of the detectives involved

191
00:12:06,456 --> 00:12:10,255
and the fact that the informant
they used was a professional.

192
00:12:10,256 --> 00:12:14,495
It sounds like you've got
a pretty good fresh evidence case.

193
00:12:14,496 --> 00:12:19,255
Have you looked at Their Lordships'
opinion in Pendleton 2002?

194
00:12:19,256 --> 00:12:22,075
I wish you were doing this one.

195
00:12:23,296 --> 00:12:24,842
What's wrong, Jo?

196
00:12:27,216 --> 00:12:28,216
Don't know.

197
00:12:29,656 --> 00:12:30,656
Jo.

198
00:12:31,616 --> 00:12:32,980
Doesn't matter.

199
00:12:41,136 --> 00:12:43,175
How's Charlie doing in your chambers?

200
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:44,736
She works hard.

201
00:12:48,456 --> 00:12:50,895
The fact is, I've made up my mind
to take on Michael Hulsey,

202
00:12:50,896 --> 00:12:52,624
if they'll have me.

203
00:12:52,936 --> 00:12:55,015
What happened to the decent
foster parents?

204
00:12:55,016 --> 00:12:57,815
Why is it that no one
wants me to do this?

205
00:12:57,816 --> 00:13:00,815
I'm just asking. Are you
going to give up a brilliant career?

206
00:13:00,816 --> 00:13:03,215
- It's a sabbatical.
- 'Cause you could be a judge next year.

207
00:13:03,216 --> 00:13:04,375
I thought that was all you wanted.

208
00:13:04,376 --> 00:13:06,335
That seems to be what you want.
I don't know any more.

209
00:13:06,336 --> 00:13:08,175
Jo, anybody can be a mother.

210
00:13:08,176 --> 00:13:10,813
So speaks the perfect father?

211
00:13:11,016 --> 00:13:12,975
It's stupid to give away
what you've got.

212
00:13:12,976 --> 00:13:14,249
Yes, isn't it?

213
00:13:14,376 --> 00:13:17,055
To guide a damaged little life into some

214
00:13:17,056 --> 00:13:19,295
harbour of warmth and affection.

215
00:13:19,296 --> 00:13:21,215
I've got no problem with
what you've described.

216
00:13:21,216 --> 00:13:23,853
Just let somebody else do it.

217
00:13:35,336 --> 00:13:37,791
CHARLIE: Jo, you all right?

218
00:13:37,896 --> 00:13:40,015
You look like you've seen a ghost.

219
00:13:40,016 --> 00:13:41,835
Yes, I think I have.

220
00:13:42,736 --> 00:13:43,966
Who are they?

221
00:13:45,256 --> 00:13:47,855
The one on the right is a very corrupt
ex-Detective Sergeant

222
00:13:47,856 --> 00:13:49,616
named Troy Romano.

223
00:13:49,696 --> 00:13:50,935
The other one's probably
just as corrupt.

224
00:13:50,936 --> 00:13:53,895
They're the ones who arrested
Denny McDowell.

225
00:13:53,896 --> 00:13:56,415
Well, what are they doing here?

226
00:13:56,416 --> 00:14:01,689
Checking their corrupt efforts
aren't overturned, I expect.

227
00:14:07,496 --> 00:14:10,695
I would urge Your Lordships to give
particular weight in this appeal

228
00:14:10,696 --> 00:14:14,455
to the new evidence revealed
by subsequent DNA profiling.

229
00:14:14,456 --> 00:14:17,215
It strongly suggests that
the appellant, Mr McDowell,

230
00:14:17,216 --> 00:14:20,495
was not at the scene of the crime
as the spent cigarette

231
00:14:20,496 --> 00:14:24,095
had previously and erroneously
indicated.

232
00:14:24,096 --> 00:14:26,375
Whilst there's no doubt
that the cigarette was there.

233
00:14:26,376 --> 00:14:28,695
The new DNA profiling clearly shows

234
00:14:28,696 --> 00:14:32,242
that it wasn't, in fact, smoked by him.

235
00:14:32,896 --> 00:14:36,735
In the Queen and Pendleton, 2002,
Their Lordships held,

236
00:14:36,736 --> 00:14:39,135
"where fresh evidence is admitted,

237
00:14:39,136 --> 00:14:42,135
"the Court of Appeal
has to make a judgement

238
00:14:42,136 --> 00:14:44,895
"whether the conviction is unsafe."

239
00:14:44,896 --> 00:14:48,375
I would further remind Your Lordships,
should you need reminding,

240
00:14:48,376 --> 00:14:50,255
that the proven dishonesty
of the arresting officers

241
00:14:50,256 --> 00:14:54,135
undermines their credibility
as key witnesses in the original trial.

242
00:14:54,136 --> 00:14:56,015
Detective Sergeant Troy Romano

243
00:14:56,016 --> 00:14:59,655
had subsequently been arrested
and successfully prosecuted

244
00:14:59,656 --> 00:15:02,375
for perverting the course of justice.

245
00:15:02,376 --> 00:15:04,575
He was sent to prison for five years.

246
00:15:04,576 --> 00:15:08,135
Are we to assume all Mr Romano's arrests

247
00:15:08,136 --> 00:15:12,655
to be tainted
because of one black mark, Mrs Mills?

248
00:15:12,656 --> 00:15:14,055
I would point Your Lordships

249
00:15:14,056 --> 00:15:17,815
to the most recent European
human rights jurisprudence.

250
00:15:17,816 --> 00:15:20,175
It urges that all convictions

251
00:15:20,176 --> 00:15:23,735
where policemen have
subsequently been prosecuted

252
00:15:23,736 --> 00:15:25,736
should be re-examined.

253
00:15:26,776 --> 00:15:29,295
Pendleton suggests, "It's wise to test

254
00:15:29,296 --> 00:15:32,735
"whether the new evidence,
if given at trial,

255
00:15:32,736 --> 00:15:38,918
"might reasonably have affected
the decision of the jury to convict."

256
00:15:38,976 --> 00:15:41,815
Thank you. Thank you, Mrs Mills.

257
00:15:41,816 --> 00:15:45,453
There's quite a lot of law
involved here.

258
00:15:45,816 --> 00:15:50,415
For that reason, we will retire
to consider our finding.

259
00:15:50,416 --> 00:15:51,871
USHER: All rise.

260
00:15:55,616 --> 00:15:58,095
- What do you think, Jo?
- I haven't a clue.

261
00:15:58,096 --> 00:15:59,495
He's your granddad.

262
00:15:59,496 --> 00:16:05,215
Sometimes it's hard to remember that
when he's sitting up there.

263
00:16:05,216 --> 00:16:08,575
I'm not minded to say
that the conviction is unsafe.

264
00:16:08,576 --> 00:16:10,395
If we are satisfied,

265
00:16:10,456 --> 00:16:14,056
despite any irregularities in the trial

266
00:16:14,136 --> 00:16:16,975
or any new evidence
appearing subsequently,

267
00:16:16,976 --> 00:16:20,015
that the conviction is still safe,

268
00:16:20,016 --> 00:16:23,107
then we should dismiss the appeal.

269
00:16:23,456 --> 00:16:27,015
Where I hesitate over
the safety of the conviction

270
00:16:27,016 --> 00:16:30,695
is on considering the records of
the police officers who were involved.

271
00:16:30,696 --> 00:16:32,615
Only one of them
behaved incorrectly, Monty.

272
00:16:32,616 --> 00:16:35,535
We might assume that the others
were also tainted.

273
00:16:35,536 --> 00:16:39,095
Oh, it was one incident in an otherwise
exemplary career.

274
00:16:39,096 --> 00:16:40,735
Why should we assume otherwise?

275
00:16:40,736 --> 00:16:43,535
One has to look at
the law of probability, Joe.

276
00:16:43,536 --> 00:16:46,855
Yet so many get away with it
and then sue the police.

277
00:16:46,856 --> 00:16:51,215
I'm beginning to feel like that Dutch
boy with this finger in the dike.

278
00:16:51,216 --> 00:16:54,216
Well, what do you think, Michael?

279
00:16:54,376 --> 00:16:57,015
Well, I wasn't convinced
by the new evidence

280
00:16:57,016 --> 00:16:58,575
or the argument, Monty.

281
00:16:58,576 --> 00:17:02,135
We can't possibly declare
open season on the police service.

282
00:17:02,136 --> 00:17:05,695
Convicted criminals will be
queuing up to draw on the public purse

283
00:17:05,696 --> 00:17:09,969
and further stretch the resources
of the courts.

284
00:17:14,336 --> 00:17:16,700
I'll make a call about it.

285
00:17:19,096 --> 00:17:21,295
I suppose you think
that's a wonderful result.

286
00:17:21,296 --> 00:17:23,055
- What are you talking about?
- You know very well.

287
00:17:23,056 --> 00:17:24,695
I'm gonna go on working
on the McDowell appeal

288
00:17:24,696 --> 00:17:26,935
and I will prove that you
fabricated the evidence.

289
00:17:26,936 --> 00:17:28,935
(LAUGHING) Well, good luck.

290
00:17:28,936 --> 00:17:30,935
- Why don't you let it go, Mrs Mills?
- No.

291
00:17:30,936 --> 00:17:32,815
You will not get away
with your corruption.

292
00:17:32,816 --> 00:17:36,495
- Well, we just did, didn't we?
- MASSEY: Troy.

293
00:17:36,496 --> 00:17:37,936
Look at her.

294
00:17:39,776 --> 00:17:44,140
She'd like me to take a pop at her,
wouldn't she?

295
00:17:48,136 --> 00:17:50,495
Wouldn't like to get
on the wrong side of him.

296
00:17:50,496 --> 00:17:53,496
I will find a way of potting him.

297
00:17:59,456 --> 00:18:00,576
Mrs Mills.

298
00:18:01,216 --> 00:18:02,398
Thanks, Coop.

299
00:18:02,416 --> 00:18:04,935
Your directions hearing
is still waiting.

300
00:18:04,936 --> 00:18:07,027
Yes, I know. Thank you.

301
00:18:10,256 --> 00:18:12,815
Is she cross about something?

302
00:18:12,816 --> 00:18:16,935
- You should be elsewhere.
- I won't take up much of your time.

303
00:18:16,936 --> 00:18:19,175
I read the papers
in the Terry Rogers defence.

304
00:18:19,176 --> 00:18:22,255
DS Romano and his colleagues
were deeply implicated in corruption.

305
00:18:22,256 --> 00:18:23,855
Only according to the defendant.

306
00:18:23,856 --> 00:18:26,295
No, I believe it.
It all makes such sense.

307
00:18:26,296 --> 00:18:28,815
You know what they were like
when the arrested Denny McDowell.

308
00:18:28,816 --> 00:18:31,415
The Rogers arrest is exactly similar.

309
00:18:31,416 --> 00:18:35,871
Does this mean you're not
taking a sabbatical, Jo?

310
00:18:36,016 --> 00:18:38,215
I'm not giving up adopting Michael,
if that's what you mean.

311
00:18:38,216 --> 00:18:40,695
I know it's the right thing to do.

312
00:18:40,696 --> 00:18:41,696
I do, John.

313
00:18:41,776 --> 00:18:44,055
How do you think you're gonna
manage to look after this boy

314
00:18:44,056 --> 00:18:46,775
and still run a case you think
reeks of injustice?

315
00:18:46,776 --> 00:18:49,335
- You managed all right with Charlie.
- It's different.

316
00:18:49,336 --> 00:18:51,415
Michael will need a lot of attention,
a lot of support.

317
00:18:51,416 --> 00:18:53,144
And Charlie didn't?

318
00:18:54,456 --> 00:18:55,456
(SIGHS)

319
00:19:11,976 --> 00:19:12,976
Mrs Mills.

320
00:19:26,456 --> 00:19:27,911
(MOBILE RINGING)

321
00:19:29,256 --> 00:19:30,256
Jo Mills.

322
00:19:30,336 --> 00:19:32,455
This is Sue Bush,
Michael's house mother.

323
00:19:32,456 --> 00:19:33,456
Hello.

324
00:19:33,536 --> 00:19:36,695
Michael was asking if you're coming
to see him after school today.

325
00:19:36,696 --> 00:19:38,424
Yes, I'm gonna try.

326
00:19:38,936 --> 00:19:41,166
Mrs Mills... Jo,

327
00:19:41,256 --> 00:19:45,055
I don't wanna be pushy, but you
have to say one way or the other.

328
00:19:45,056 --> 00:19:47,975
These children have
enough uncertainty in their lives.

329
00:19:47,976 --> 00:19:50,415
I'm sorry. Of course I'll be there.

330
00:19:50,416 --> 00:19:51,598
I have to go.

331
00:19:57,136 --> 00:19:59,335
How'd you come to be teaching?

332
00:19:59,336 --> 00:20:00,791
Did you qualify?

333
00:20:02,096 --> 00:20:04,824
No, it was purely by accident.

334
00:20:06,256 --> 00:20:09,295
After I went on the run,
I fetched up in Devon.

335
00:20:09,296 --> 00:20:12,495
Did a bit of casual work on the boats.

336
00:20:12,496 --> 00:20:15,496
I was always quite good on water.

337
00:20:16,256 --> 00:20:18,295
Someone I was working with

338
00:20:18,296 --> 00:20:22,135
asked if I wanted to help at
a canoeing club on the Dart.

339
00:20:22,136 --> 00:20:26,318
It wasn't paid work,
but they were nice people.

340
00:20:29,056 --> 00:20:32,695
I suppose if it had been paid, I might
have just drifted back onto the drugs.

341
00:20:32,696 --> 00:20:35,696
Had you been clean all the while?

342
00:20:36,056 --> 00:20:37,056
Yeah.

343
00:20:38,256 --> 00:20:40,984
Since being in police custody.

344
00:20:42,216 --> 00:20:43,695
A very sobering experience,

345
00:20:43,696 --> 00:20:47,969
being arrested for robbery
and attempted murder.

346
00:20:50,256 --> 00:20:52,347
I was good with canoes.

347
00:20:53,216 --> 00:20:55,735
Then one of the organisers said
he was starting up a club at the school

348
00:20:55,736 --> 00:20:57,455
and asked if I wanted to help out.

349
00:20:57,456 --> 00:21:00,456
That led to some paid work there.

350
00:21:00,656 --> 00:21:03,895
There were no cheques
back in those days.

351
00:21:03,896 --> 00:21:05,855
Working with decent people
made me realise

352
00:21:05,856 --> 00:21:08,775
what a waste all that other scene
had been.

353
00:21:08,776 --> 00:21:12,504
- Where had you been living?
- In hostels.

354
00:21:13,376 --> 00:21:15,558
Sometimes I slept rough.

355
00:21:15,776 --> 00:21:18,015
I was making hardly anything
when I worked on the canoeing.

356
00:21:18,016 --> 00:21:19,815
Wasn't that a problem,

357
00:21:19,816 --> 00:21:23,975
mixing with normal people
at the club and the school?

358
00:21:23,976 --> 00:21:24,976
Yeah.

359
00:21:26,816 --> 00:21:30,135
I tried to keep myself clean and tidy.

360
00:21:30,136 --> 00:21:33,335
I used to go to the washroom
in the station.

361
00:21:33,336 --> 00:21:37,575
Then one of the older teachers
cottoned on when she caught me

362
00:21:37,576 --> 00:21:39,135
washing my clothes in the school
cloak room

363
00:21:39,136 --> 00:21:41,775
and drying them on the radiator.

364
00:21:41,776 --> 00:21:43,415
I said I got wet in the river.

365
00:21:43,416 --> 00:21:48,326
She said, "You must have had
a lot of clothes on then."

366
00:21:51,016 --> 00:21:53,926
She gave me a room in her place.

367
00:21:56,976 --> 00:22:01,886
Even gave me a key
so I could come and go when I liked.

368
00:22:06,376 --> 00:22:10,467
I'd been on the run
two-and-a-half years then.

369
00:22:13,976 --> 00:22:18,255
You have no idea what it's like,
Mrs Mills, to be finally

370
00:22:18,256 --> 00:22:20,893
treated decently, like you're

371
00:22:21,896 --> 00:22:24,987
a human being that has some worth.

372
00:22:28,896 --> 00:22:32,655
I knew then that nothing would
drag me back to my former life.

373
00:22:32,656 --> 00:22:36,566
How did you come to be caught
by the police?

374
00:22:38,816 --> 00:22:39,816
I had...

375
00:22:42,536 --> 00:22:46,264
I'd been thinking a lot
about my daughter.

376
00:22:49,376 --> 00:22:52,467
It was her 18th birthday. I, uh...

377
00:22:52,776 --> 00:22:56,655
I tried to contact her.
I knew it was risky,

378
00:22:56,656 --> 00:22:57,838
but I had to.

379
00:23:03,216 --> 00:23:08,307
I felt really bad about what
I'd done to her and her mum.

380
00:23:18,896 --> 00:23:23,256
It was a real shock when I turned up.

381
00:23:27,736 --> 00:23:32,827
Katie couldn't talk to me.
She just went off to her room.

382
00:23:34,056 --> 00:23:36,329
Must have been the shock.

383
00:23:37,176 --> 00:23:40,455
Cheryl had done a great job
of bringing her up.

384
00:23:40,456 --> 00:23:43,135
Your wife had divorced you
while you were on the run?

385
00:23:43,136 --> 00:23:46,864
Yeah, she should have done it
before then.

386
00:23:46,896 --> 00:23:51,078
I was just dragging her through
all my rubbish.

387
00:23:52,496 --> 00:23:54,406
Seeing them, I, um...

388
00:23:54,776 --> 00:23:58,295
I thought I could somehow
be reconciled with my daughter.

389
00:23:58,296 --> 00:24:01,935
I even imagined I could get
back together with Cheryl.

390
00:24:01,936 --> 00:24:05,209
So I thought about giving myself up.

391
00:24:05,256 --> 00:24:07,376
I'd read somewhere that

392
00:24:07,456 --> 00:24:10,415
you could approach the Director
of Public Prosecutions

393
00:24:10,416 --> 00:24:14,735
and, based on the new life you'd been
leading, negotiate your return.

394
00:24:14,736 --> 00:24:16,535
Did you do that? I didn't read it here.

395
00:24:16,536 --> 00:24:18,355
I did approach them.

396
00:24:19,176 --> 00:24:22,449
I just wanted so badly to be normal.

397
00:24:23,136 --> 00:24:26,415
They said that they would be interested
in meeting with me

398
00:24:26,416 --> 00:24:29,144
to try and clear this case up.

399
00:24:29,656 --> 00:24:33,616
The next thing I knew,
the police showed up.

400
00:24:38,856 --> 00:24:40,311
Why did you run?

401
00:24:42,016 --> 00:24:44,816
I don't know. I got scared.

402
00:24:47,096 --> 00:24:49,278
The DPP sent the police?

403
00:24:49,656 --> 00:24:50,726
I suppose.

404
00:24:51,736 --> 00:24:56,464
Doesn't make sense if they
were negotiating with you.

405
00:24:56,856 --> 00:25:00,295
Or maybe the police were watching
Cheryl's house and followed me.

406
00:25:00,296 --> 00:25:02,975
I doubt it. Not after 16 years.

407
00:25:02,976 --> 00:25:06,095
Possibly someone
from the DPP betrayed you.

408
00:25:06,096 --> 00:25:08,551
Why was that, do you think?

409
00:25:11,256 --> 00:25:12,438
I don't know.

410
00:25:12,456 --> 00:25:15,695
Is there something
you're not telling me?

411
00:25:15,696 --> 00:25:16,696
No.

412
00:25:34,176 --> 00:25:37,995
I am going to take on your case,
Mr Rogers.

413
00:25:39,936 --> 00:25:41,575
I can't tell you what it means
to him, Mrs Mills,

414
00:25:41,576 --> 00:25:43,375
having someone like you believe in him.

415
00:25:43,376 --> 00:25:46,295
There are holes in his case.
Questions that need answering.

416
00:25:46,296 --> 00:25:50,135
He doesn't seem to have any evidence
to corroborate his claims.

417
00:25:50,136 --> 00:25:52,495
We need someone to find the answers.

418
00:25:52,496 --> 00:25:54,255
There's an ex-policeman
that we sometimes use,

419
00:25:54,256 --> 00:25:55,935
but I'm not sure he'd be right
for something like this.

420
00:25:55,936 --> 00:25:58,615
There's a junior we can use
in chambers, Charlie Deed.

421
00:25:58,616 --> 00:26:01,255
With luck, I'll knock this out
at the pre-trial hearing.

422
00:26:01,256 --> 00:26:04,256
(SIGHS) It'll mean a lot of work.

423
00:26:27,296 --> 00:26:30,056
Jo, this might do it.

424
00:26:31,416 --> 00:26:34,015
Since the Gladstone report in 1895,

425
00:26:34,016 --> 00:26:36,575
the aim of the prison system
has been to encourage

426
00:26:36,576 --> 00:26:40,455
and assist prisoners
to lead a good and useful life.

427
00:26:40,456 --> 00:26:44,215
Well, Terry Rogers has achieved that
without the need for custody.

428
00:26:44,216 --> 00:26:45,216
Brilliant.

429
00:26:55,696 --> 00:26:57,615
You're going to be busy, Judge.

430
00:26:57,616 --> 00:27:01,455
Mr Justice Everard is going into
hospital the day after tomorrow.

431
00:27:01,456 --> 00:27:03,526
Ooh. Anything serious?

432
00:27:03,616 --> 00:27:08,255
Well, word along the corridor
is that it's a vasectomy.

433
00:27:08,256 --> 00:27:10,975
But his clerk said it was a hernia.

434
00:27:10,976 --> 00:27:11,976
Uh-huh.

435
00:27:12,336 --> 00:27:14,775
Anyway, he gave me this case.

436
00:27:14,776 --> 00:27:17,575
The 13-year-old who raped his teacher.

437
00:27:17,576 --> 00:27:18,776
It is ready?

438
00:27:18,856 --> 00:27:22,493
Not yet, Judge.
They're awaiting reports.

439
00:27:22,776 --> 00:27:25,975
JO: To stay at large and functioning
for 16 years is,

440
00:27:25,976 --> 00:27:29,055
I hope you'll agree, my Lord,
a remarkable achievement.

441
00:27:29,056 --> 00:27:32,615
Especially when this is done
without resorting to crime.

442
00:27:32,616 --> 00:27:36,895
In addition, Mr Rogers has led
an exemplary life.

443
00:27:36,896 --> 00:27:41,615
One to which many of us might aspire,
much less those outside the law.

444
00:27:41,616 --> 00:27:43,375
It is for these reasons I argue

445
00:27:43,376 --> 00:27:47,015
that Terry Rogers should not now
be on trial.

446
00:27:47,016 --> 00:27:50,575
He is a very different man
from the one whom, it's alleged,

447
00:27:50,576 --> 00:27:52,615
committed this crime
all those years ago.

448
00:27:52,616 --> 00:27:57,162
My Lord, I cannot emphasise
"alleged" too strongly.

449
00:27:58,296 --> 00:28:01,255
One of the arresting officers
in this case was a thoroughly bad lot

450
00:28:01,256 --> 00:28:04,415
and went to prison for corruption
and perverting the course of justice.

451
00:28:04,416 --> 00:28:08,735
But the other three detectives involved
have hitherto impeccable records,

452
00:28:08,736 --> 00:28:09,827
do they not?

453
00:28:09,896 --> 00:28:12,695
The principle which underpins
criminal justice

454
00:28:12,696 --> 00:28:15,655
is that everyone and anyone
is capable of reforming,

455
00:28:15,656 --> 00:28:18,415
and having reformed,
deserves a second chance.

456
00:28:18,416 --> 00:28:21,415
We would argue that Mr Rogers
is a changed man

457
00:28:21,416 --> 00:28:23,615
and deserves a second chance.

458
00:28:23,616 --> 00:28:26,415
The principle can only be adopted
subsequent to dealing

459
00:28:26,416 --> 00:28:28,935
with the original index crime.

460
00:28:28,936 --> 00:28:31,495
Mr Rogers does not absolve himself
of the crime

461
00:28:31,496 --> 00:28:34,135
simply by absconding for 16 years,

462
00:28:34,136 --> 00:28:37,655
however virtuous his behaviour
during his flight from justice.

463
00:28:37,656 --> 00:28:41,295
Mr Rogers alleges that the detective
who was convicted for corruption

464
00:28:41,296 --> 00:28:45,695
put a plastic bag over his head in order
to force him to confess.

465
00:28:45,696 --> 00:28:49,775
This is exactly similar to the case
where the detective was convicted.

466
00:28:49,776 --> 00:28:52,655
Well, then, doubtless you will bring
that forward as evidence

467
00:28:52,656 --> 00:28:56,046
and argue it at Mr Rogers' trial.

468
00:28:56,136 --> 00:28:59,975
Now, are you ready for this case to be
given a fixed date for trial?

469
00:28:59,976 --> 00:29:01,895
Would you hear an argument on
public policy

470
00:29:01,896 --> 00:29:04,015
in the interests of justice
for Mr Rogers?

471
00:29:04,016 --> 00:29:08,175
Not if it avoids taking responsibility
for the original crime.

472
00:29:08,176 --> 00:29:10,255
Are you ready for trial?

473
00:29:10,256 --> 00:29:14,815
We are, my Lord. However, I would ask
that you hear an application for bail.

474
00:29:14,816 --> 00:29:18,695
Mr Rogers has proved
a reliable and trustworthy citizen.

475
00:29:18,696 --> 00:29:22,735
Further, he did approach the DPP
to negotiate his return.

476
00:29:22,736 --> 00:29:26,135
Well, that would be to risk
waiting another 16 years

477
00:29:26,136 --> 00:29:29,695
for this matter to be dealt with. No,
I don't think that bail is appropriate,

478
00:29:29,696 --> 00:29:30,696
Mrs Mills.

479
00:29:31,696 --> 00:29:34,255
Sir Maurice, is your case ready?

480
00:29:34,256 --> 00:29:36,695
Aside from one matter, my Lord.

481
00:29:36,696 --> 00:29:39,935
The Crown will require
an ex parte hearing

482
00:29:39,936 --> 00:29:44,375
to raise an issue relating to
an informant and the public interest.

483
00:29:44,376 --> 00:29:46,815
I will not be hearing the case
personally

484
00:29:46,816 --> 00:29:49,456
due to a prior commitment.

485
00:29:49,776 --> 00:29:52,135
It is being passed to Mr Justice Deed,

486
00:29:52,136 --> 00:29:55,855
whose clerk will notify you
when he's available to hear it.

487
00:29:55,856 --> 00:29:57,456
Approach him.

488
00:30:01,136 --> 00:30:02,815
Did you know they had
an informant in this case?

489
00:30:02,816 --> 00:30:05,255
Terry didn't say that's how
he was caught originally, no.

490
00:30:05,256 --> 00:30:06,415
Well, it's obviously what happened.

491
00:30:06,416 --> 00:30:08,215
What's the prosecution
after with the judge?

492
00:30:08,216 --> 00:30:10,215
To make sure the name
of the informant doesn't get out.

493
00:30:10,216 --> 00:30:12,655
- Can they do that?
- Of course, if the judge lets them.

494
00:30:12,656 --> 00:30:14,295
Sir Maurice is so paranoid about it,

495
00:30:14,296 --> 00:30:17,135
he won't even risk me being
at the hearing with the judge.

496
00:30:17,136 --> 00:30:19,175
We badly need to find someone
who can corroborate

497
00:30:19,176 --> 00:30:22,255
what Terry Rogers said the police did.

498
00:30:22,256 --> 00:30:23,802
I've got to dash.

499
00:30:28,456 --> 00:30:30,455
To be honest, Mrs Mills,

500
00:30:30,456 --> 00:30:32,255
I think it would be best for Michael
long-term

501
00:30:32,256 --> 00:30:34,615
if you were to abandon the idea
of adoption.

502
00:30:34,616 --> 00:30:41,566
No. No. I want to give Michael a home,
but I have to do this case first.

503
00:30:41,936 --> 00:30:44,815
It seems to me his life with you
would be a perpetual seesaw.

504
00:30:44,816 --> 00:30:48,362
It'll be different when Michael's here.

505
00:30:49,976 --> 00:30:52,895
I understand you need
this operation, Monty.

506
00:30:52,896 --> 00:30:56,535
But all being well, you'll be back
in a couple of weeks or so.

507
00:30:56,536 --> 00:30:59,575
I'd really rather not take on
this Terry Rogers case.

508
00:30:59,576 --> 00:31:02,535
One of the detectives in that case
was also involved in a case

509
00:31:02,536 --> 00:31:05,255
that I defended,
and I know him to be corrupt.

510
00:31:05,256 --> 00:31:09,935
Oh, I'm sure we can rely upon you
not to be biased, John,

511
00:31:09,936 --> 00:31:12,575
even if it is Mrs Mills defending.

512
00:31:12,576 --> 00:31:13,576
Oh, Monty.

513
00:31:14,176 --> 00:31:16,722
There is nothing between us.

514
00:31:16,856 --> 00:31:20,295
Unless you include her asking my advice
about this boy she wants to adopt.

515
00:31:20,296 --> 00:31:23,215
She ought to have more sense at her age.

516
00:31:23,216 --> 00:31:27,775
MONTY: I can see no serious conflict
of interest in either aspect.

517
00:31:27,776 --> 00:31:29,895
I mean, we've all had
policemen before us

518
00:31:29,896 --> 00:31:34,896
who've been involved in cases
we've previously defended.

519
00:31:36,296 --> 00:31:40,895
MAURICE: The crime that the defendant,
Terry Rogers, is charged with

520
00:31:40,896 --> 00:31:44,735
took place many years ago,
members of the jury.

521
00:31:44,736 --> 00:31:49,282
But that does not lessen its
importance to the law.

522
00:31:49,376 --> 00:31:52,615
The defence may try
to make much of this gap,

523
00:31:52,616 --> 00:31:55,775
and to query witnesses
as to their memories,

524
00:31:55,776 --> 00:32:00,695
but to the man who was stabbed
during this violent robbery

525
00:32:00,696 --> 00:32:02,815
and who subsequently lost a kidney,

526
00:32:02,816 --> 00:32:07,362
the memory is as clear
and terrifying as yesterday.

527
00:32:08,576 --> 00:32:10,535
The defence may try to highlight

528
00:32:10,536 --> 00:32:14,536
Terry Rogers' alleged
current good character.

529
00:32:14,696 --> 00:32:17,576
But justice must punish crimes,

530
00:32:18,056 --> 00:32:20,875
even those buried deep in dust.

531
00:32:21,696 --> 00:32:24,855
Before you in the dock, you see a man

532
00:32:24,856 --> 00:32:30,038
who was a violent, self-abusing,
wife-abusing drug addict,

533
00:32:31,176 --> 00:32:35,631
who indulged in crime to feed
that wretched habit.

534
00:32:36,536 --> 00:32:40,695
One who often associated with others
far out of his league in criminal terms,

535
00:32:40,696 --> 00:32:45,216
but who he respected enough
never to betray.

536
00:32:46,736 --> 00:32:51,295
Four armed men carried out
the robbery on the supermarket,

537
00:32:51,296 --> 00:32:55,295
wounding the manager, Simon Estevez,
with a knife,

538
00:32:55,296 --> 00:33:00,478
and making off with ã13,000,
none of which was recovered.

539
00:33:01,656 --> 00:33:04,126
Following a heroin binge,

540
00:33:04,696 --> 00:33:06,935
the defendant was arrested

541
00:33:06,936 --> 00:33:13,209
and eventually signed a statement
confessing to his part in the crime.

542
00:33:13,976 --> 00:33:18,095
Soon after this, he managed
to escape from police custody.

543
00:33:18,096 --> 00:33:21,295
Then, without a single qualification,

544
00:33:21,296 --> 00:33:24,376
he got a job teaching children,

545
00:33:25,136 --> 00:33:30,318
living with false papers
under the name of Hayden Russell.

546
00:33:30,576 --> 00:33:35,255
I don't know how to get
false papers, members of the jury,

547
00:33:35,256 --> 00:33:38,535
and I suspect that none of you
do either.

548
00:33:38,536 --> 00:33:43,175
Only someone with the right connections,
such as Terry Rogers,

549
00:33:43,176 --> 00:33:44,904
knows these things.

550
00:33:46,736 --> 00:33:48,918
Superintendent Craddock.

551
00:33:49,816 --> 00:33:53,535
Could you tell the court
how long you were with the crime squad?

552
00:33:53,536 --> 00:33:54,935
Seven years, sir.

553
00:33:54,936 --> 00:33:57,215
And what was the nature of the work
you did?

554
00:33:57,216 --> 00:33:59,895
We pursued armed robbers, sir.

555
00:33:59,896 --> 00:34:02,735
MAURICE: Was that dangerous work,
Superintendent?

556
00:34:02,736 --> 00:34:05,055
CRADDOCK:
It could be quite dangerous.

557
00:34:05,056 --> 00:34:07,775
I was shot on two different occasions.

558
00:34:07,776 --> 00:34:12,215
MAURICE: Are you being modest
when you say, "Quite dangerous"?

559
00:34:12,216 --> 00:34:16,307
You don't have to answer that,
Superintendent.

560
00:34:16,696 --> 00:34:19,935
Why did you go on
with such dangerous work?

561
00:34:19,936 --> 00:34:22,375
(wHISPERING) Stop it, Maurice.

562
00:34:22,376 --> 00:34:25,255
We didn't think about it. There were
lots of armed robbers in those days.

563
00:34:25,256 --> 00:34:27,376
It was part of the job.

564
00:34:27,536 --> 00:34:30,935
Was the supermarket robbery
in which the manager,

565
00:34:30,936 --> 00:34:34,335
Mr Simon Estevez, was stabbed,
one such job?

566
00:34:34,336 --> 00:34:37,215
It was. There were 37 robberies
that year,

567
00:34:37,216 --> 00:34:40,216
all involving knives or firearms.

568
00:34:40,496 --> 00:34:46,315
Then I would say you're being
very modest indeed, Superintendent.

569
00:34:49,736 --> 00:34:53,095
How did you come to arrest the defendant
for the robbery 16 years ago?

570
00:34:53,096 --> 00:34:54,096
My Lord...

571
00:34:54,176 --> 00:34:57,813
You can't prevent defence counsel
asking.

572
00:34:59,136 --> 00:35:00,856
Superintendent.

573
00:35:00,936 --> 00:35:02,975
A tip-off from an informant.

574
00:35:02,976 --> 00:35:05,815
You seem very sure about that.
It was 16 years ago.

575
00:35:05,816 --> 00:35:07,895
He was a reliable informant.

576
00:35:07,896 --> 00:35:09,495
One that you had used before?

577
00:35:09,496 --> 00:35:12,175
He'd been used by squad members
on a number of occasions.

578
00:35:12,176 --> 00:35:13,655
What's the name of this informant?

579
00:35:13,656 --> 00:35:16,495
Mrs Mills, I have agreed to
the prosecution's request

580
00:35:16,496 --> 00:35:18,935
that this person's name
is not revealed in court.

581
00:35:18,936 --> 00:35:22,335
Then it's possible my client
won't get a fair trial.

582
00:35:22,336 --> 00:35:27,135
If you can demonstrate that,
I'll reconsider my ruling.

583
00:35:27,136 --> 00:35:30,335
There can be a number of reasons why
a name such as this

584
00:35:30,336 --> 00:35:31,791
is not revealed.

585
00:35:32,416 --> 00:35:35,935
Because he or she
is continuing to help the police,

586
00:35:35,936 --> 00:35:40,215
or because revealing it would put
this person in danger, for example.

587
00:35:40,216 --> 00:35:44,216
It's called public interest immunity,
or PII.

588
00:35:47,016 --> 00:35:49,495
Ah, Jo. Good, I've caught you.

589
00:35:49,496 --> 00:35:51,415
How far have you got
with the backgrounds of the police?

590
00:35:51,416 --> 00:35:52,975
The prosecution have been smart

591
00:35:52,976 --> 00:35:55,655
in calling only two of the four
who were involved.

592
00:35:55,656 --> 00:35:57,415
They both have straightforward records.

593
00:35:57,416 --> 00:35:59,815
Craddock has two commendations
for bravery.

594
00:35:59,816 --> 00:36:01,695
He's obviously a changed man.

595
00:36:01,696 --> 00:36:03,335
If he was ever involved.

596
00:36:03,336 --> 00:36:04,815
We know that Romano went to prison.

597
00:36:04,816 --> 00:36:07,335
The other arresting officer,
Matthew Stone,

598
00:36:07,336 --> 00:36:09,655
he was allowed to take early retirement.

599
00:36:09,656 --> 00:36:13,255
I can get them into the witness box,
but I doubt if they'll help us.

600
00:36:13,256 --> 00:36:16,415
We still haven't got anyone
who can corroborate Rogers' story.

601
00:36:16,416 --> 00:36:20,144
He hasn't got anyone.
I can't find anyone.

602
00:36:20,416 --> 00:36:22,575
Try our man in Parkhurst Prison.

603
00:36:22,576 --> 00:36:24,615
Denny McDowell.
I'm sure he can give us a start.

604
00:36:24,616 --> 00:36:28,135
Romano used
the plastic-bag trick on him.

605
00:36:28,136 --> 00:36:29,864
Go and talk to him.

606
00:36:34,176 --> 00:36:36,086
Excuse me. Mrs Mills?

607
00:36:36,136 --> 00:36:38,535
I'm Kate Rogers,
Terry Rogers's daughter.

608
00:36:38,536 --> 00:36:40,735
- Hello.
- My mum said he did it.

609
00:36:40,736 --> 00:36:43,191
He robbed that supermarket.

610
00:36:43,216 --> 00:36:45,815
How does she know? was she there?

611
00:36:45,816 --> 00:36:48,255
He might seem perfect to you,
but you don't even know him.

612
00:36:48,256 --> 00:36:50,655
He is such a liar.
It's stupid to believe him

613
00:36:50,656 --> 00:36:53,656
after everything he's done to us.

614
00:36:57,336 --> 00:37:00,935
JO: Two commendations for bravery
and promotion to superintendent

615
00:37:00,936 --> 00:37:02,456
by the age of 40

616
00:37:02,536 --> 00:37:06,335
would indicate an exemplary career,
Superintendent.

617
00:37:06,336 --> 00:37:08,295
Is yours an exemplary career?

618
00:37:08,296 --> 00:37:09,478
I do my best.

619
00:37:09,856 --> 00:37:12,935
How often has that best fallen short
of what is required?

620
00:37:12,936 --> 00:37:16,495
- That's not for me to say, miss.
- well, you must have some idea.

621
00:37:16,496 --> 00:37:19,415
For instance, have you ever
been investigated for corruption,

622
00:37:19,416 --> 00:37:21,246
or wrongdoing?

623
00:37:21,336 --> 00:37:24,895
Once for expenses irregularities,
once for alleged racism.

624
00:37:24,896 --> 00:37:26,615
Both were proven unfounded.

625
00:37:26,616 --> 00:37:30,095
So neither of them impeded
your rapid promotion?

626
00:37:30,096 --> 00:37:31,575
It would seem not, miss.

627
00:37:31,576 --> 00:37:34,735
Was that promotion as a result
of your work with the crime squad?

628
00:37:34,736 --> 00:37:37,335
I suppose
that could have been part of it.

629
00:37:37,336 --> 00:37:39,935
Were you a part of the crime squad
when it was disbanded

630
00:37:39,936 --> 00:37:41,735
because it was felt that too many
of the detectives

631
00:37:41,736 --> 00:37:44,918
were out of control or too corrupt?

632
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:49,375
Was it disbanded because it was corrupt
and out of control, Superintendent?

633
00:37:49,376 --> 00:37:51,055
I believe it was a political decision.

634
00:37:51,056 --> 00:37:54,175
It was becoming too much
like a national police force.

635
00:37:54,176 --> 00:37:57,335
Corruption was the reason given
for disbanding it.

636
00:37:57,336 --> 00:38:00,375
- You didn't think that was the case?
- No.

637
00:38:00,376 --> 00:38:03,335
Yet haven't no fewer than 28
former squad officers

638
00:38:03,336 --> 00:38:05,895
up and down the country been prosecuted?

639
00:38:05,896 --> 00:38:07,535
Not all of them have been convicted.

640
00:38:07,536 --> 00:38:10,264
How many were, Superintendent?

641
00:38:10,336 --> 00:38:11,973
I'm not sure, sir.

642
00:38:14,856 --> 00:38:17,895
But weren't detectives
as a matter of routine

643
00:38:17,896 --> 00:38:19,975
perverting the course of justice?

644
00:38:19,976 --> 00:38:24,815
Planting evidence to fit suspects up,
to use the popular jargon.

645
00:38:24,816 --> 00:38:27,775
- No. Some of that was cited.
- There was corruption.

646
00:38:27,776 --> 00:38:29,175
Not that I was aware of.

647
00:38:29,176 --> 00:38:31,415
- Weren't you part of that corruption?
- My Lord.

648
00:38:31,416 --> 00:38:34,015
This is rather flogging a dead horse.

649
00:38:34,016 --> 00:38:37,926
Mrs Mills, you know
the risk you're running.

650
00:38:41,136 --> 00:38:42,695
Superintendent, who was the informant

651
00:38:42,696 --> 00:38:45,175
who alerted you
to Terry Rogers' involvement?

652
00:38:45,176 --> 00:38:46,735
I can't tell you that, miss.

653
00:38:46,736 --> 00:38:48,806
Why? was he anonymous?

654
00:38:48,896 --> 00:38:51,535
Mrs Mills, I have ruled on this.

655
00:38:51,536 --> 00:38:54,135
My Lord, may I address you
in the absence of the jury?

656
00:38:54,136 --> 00:38:56,375
I'm surprised you hadn't
got there sooner.

657
00:38:56,376 --> 00:39:01,195
Members of the jury,
would you mind going out, please?

658
00:39:07,736 --> 00:39:11,375
I agree with you, Mrs Mills.
PII can be a loathsome device.

659
00:39:11,376 --> 00:39:14,215
But unless you can give me
a compelling argument

660
00:39:14,216 --> 00:39:16,495
for revealing this informant's identity,

661
00:39:16,496 --> 00:39:19,255
I won't ask the prosecution
to reveal it to you.

662
00:39:19,256 --> 00:39:22,455
My client is being unfairly deprived
of his liberty.

663
00:39:22,456 --> 00:39:24,535
I haven't heard anything
that indicates that.

664
00:39:24,536 --> 00:39:30,695
What about the liberty of this person
who has helped the police so often?

665
00:39:30,696 --> 00:39:32,515
I will go on trying.

666
00:39:33,816 --> 00:39:36,089
Let's have the jury back.

667
00:39:37,056 --> 00:39:40,055
JO: Superintendent, were you
one of four detectives

668
00:39:40,056 --> 00:39:42,215
who obtained a confession
from the defendant

669
00:39:42,216 --> 00:39:44,495
by placing a plastic bag over his head?

670
00:39:44,496 --> 00:39:47,775
My Lord, even if my learned friend
acknowledges the risk,

671
00:39:47,776 --> 00:39:50,255
this is an outrageous assertion.

672
00:39:50,256 --> 00:39:52,695
More coffee, members of the jury,

673
00:39:52,696 --> 00:39:55,787
whilst I have a word with counsel.

674
00:40:08,336 --> 00:40:11,295
Mrs Mills, you're an experienced
Queen's Counsel.

675
00:40:11,296 --> 00:40:14,295
If you take this line,
the prosecution will be at liberty

676
00:40:14,296 --> 00:40:18,296
to bring in the defendant's criminal
history.

677
00:40:21,376 --> 00:40:24,255
Might I have a short adjournment
to consult my client?

678
00:40:24,256 --> 00:40:25,347
Ten minutes.

679
00:40:26,296 --> 00:40:27,751
USHER: All rise.

680
00:40:32,376 --> 00:40:34,335
JO: I think I can get to
the identity of the informant

681
00:40:34,336 --> 00:40:37,855
by attacking these policemen,
but the risk is

682
00:40:37,856 --> 00:40:42,215
the prosecution can reveal
facts about your past.

683
00:40:42,216 --> 00:40:45,335
It's a gamble.
It won't paint a pretty picture.

684
00:40:45,336 --> 00:40:47,427
What choice do we have?

685
00:40:49,296 --> 00:40:52,335
- Is there anything you haven't told me?
- No.

686
00:40:52,336 --> 00:40:54,135
- All that is behind me.
- Are you sure?

687
00:40:54,136 --> 00:40:55,335
- Yes.
- Your daughter approached...

688
00:40:55,336 --> 00:40:57,791
I know. I saw her in court.

689
00:40:58,056 --> 00:41:00,895
She's very angry with me.
So is her mother.

690
00:41:00,896 --> 00:41:05,255
Nothing that comes out in court
can make them more angry.

691
00:41:05,256 --> 00:41:06,936
There is nothing.

692
00:41:13,296 --> 00:41:14,455
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

693
00:41:14,456 --> 00:41:15,606
Come.

694
00:41:15,696 --> 00:41:18,815
It was a complete waste of time
going to Parkhurst.

695
00:41:18,816 --> 00:41:21,375
Denny McDowell wouldn't talk to me.
He'll only talk to you.

696
00:41:21,376 --> 00:41:22,735
Did you tell him what I'm in court with?

697
00:41:22,736 --> 00:41:24,535
I couldn't get anywhere with him.

698
00:41:24,536 --> 00:41:26,173
He's really angry.

699
00:41:26,216 --> 00:41:29,015
Well, I suppose if you spend
a third of your life in prison

700
00:41:29,016 --> 00:41:33,295
for something you didn't do,
it would make you a little tetchy.

701
00:41:33,296 --> 00:41:34,935
I'll go and see him tomorrow.

702
00:41:34,936 --> 00:41:36,846
What about the trial?

703
00:41:37,896 --> 00:41:39,260
You'll be here.

704
00:41:40,456 --> 00:41:44,255
I'll go overnight
and see him first thing in the morning.

705
00:41:44,256 --> 00:41:47,855
It's the examination of chief
of Detective Sergeant Massey tomorrow.

706
00:41:47,856 --> 00:41:50,335
You need only take notes
of his answers to guide my cross,

707
00:41:50,336 --> 00:41:52,882
not Sir Maurice's questions.

708
00:41:54,096 --> 00:41:55,096
Jo.

709
00:42:20,016 --> 00:42:22,055
Mrs Tredaway, did we have a meeting?

710
00:42:22,056 --> 00:42:24,495
No. You had a meeting arranged
with Michael.

711
00:42:24,496 --> 00:42:26,575
Oh, God, I did. I'm sorry.

712
00:42:26,576 --> 00:42:28,335
Right after court, something came up.

713
00:42:28,336 --> 00:42:30,575
I got a call from his house mother.

714
00:42:30,576 --> 00:42:34,015
- He doesn't want to see you any more.
- what?

715
00:42:34,016 --> 00:42:35,976
No. He must.

716
00:42:36,456 --> 00:42:37,456
I want him.

717
00:42:38,656 --> 00:42:39,895
I'll go there right now.

718
00:42:39,896 --> 00:42:42,095
You won't, Mrs Mills.
Not unless Michael asks to see you.

719
00:42:42,096 --> 00:42:43,455
But I can't see that he would, can you?

720
00:42:43,456 --> 00:42:45,575
Please don't make me feel worse
than I already do.

721
00:42:45,576 --> 00:42:46,655
I want to go and see him.

722
00:42:46,656 --> 00:42:48,575
- You weren't gonna take any more cases.
- I didn't say that.

723
00:42:48,576 --> 00:42:50,575
I have to do this.
This is about an innocent man's liberty.

724
00:42:50,576 --> 00:42:52,335
Yes, and Michael is about
the rest of his life.

725
00:42:52,336 --> 00:42:54,975
I'll go there. I'll explain,
tell him how sorry I am.

726
00:42:54,976 --> 00:42:59,249
- Please, let me explain.
- He doesn't want you.

727
00:42:59,376 --> 00:43:01,136
(ENGINE STARTING)

728
00:43:21,696 --> 00:43:22,696
Come on.

729
00:43:25,376 --> 00:43:26,935
- I have to see Michael.
- You can't.

730
00:43:26,936 --> 00:43:28,815
No, you don't understand.
I have to explain.

731
00:43:28,816 --> 00:43:30,895
- You must let me see him.
- I was told you can't.

732
00:43:30,896 --> 00:43:33,135
Why are you punishing me?
If you punish me, you punish Michael.

733
00:43:33,136 --> 00:43:34,896
Michael. Michael.

734
00:43:34,976 --> 00:43:38,015
You're not doing your cause any good,
or his. I mean...

735
00:43:38,016 --> 00:43:40,175
Please, Michael. Let me explain.

736
00:43:40,176 --> 00:43:42,895
I'm sorry I'm late. You're much more
important than my work.

737
00:43:42,896 --> 00:43:44,695
You're more important than anything.

738
00:43:44,696 --> 00:43:46,878
I just... I got tied up.

739
00:43:55,616 --> 00:43:56,616
I'm sorry.

740
00:44:05,256 --> 00:44:06,711
Sergeant Massey,

741
00:44:07,336 --> 00:44:13,255
how many arrests have you made
in your long and distinguished career?

742
00:44:13,256 --> 00:44:15,695
I don't exactly know, sir,
I don't keep score.

743
00:44:15,696 --> 00:44:20,056
Would it be dozens, hundreds, thousands?

744
00:44:20,136 --> 00:44:22,455
Upper hundreds, lower thousands,
probably.

745
00:44:22,456 --> 00:44:25,455
So, without being immodest,
it would be fair to say

746
00:44:25,456 --> 00:44:29,495
that you have an impeccable record
as a police officer.

747
00:44:29,496 --> 00:44:32,535
- Yes, sir.
- A record I'm sure you wouldn't want

748
00:44:32,536 --> 00:44:37,082
- to sully by ever behaving corruptly?
- Miss Deed?

749
00:44:38,936 --> 00:44:40,535
Is your leader not in court?

750
00:44:40,536 --> 00:44:43,895
No, she's investigating
some new evidence, my Lord.

751
00:44:43,896 --> 00:44:46,624
Left you to hold the fort, eh?

752
00:44:46,656 --> 00:44:51,475
Are you challenging Sir Maurice's
line of questioning?

753
00:44:52,456 --> 00:44:54,286
Should I, my Lord?

754
00:44:54,376 --> 00:44:57,655
Yes, I'd say he was leading the witness,
wouldn't you?

755
00:44:57,656 --> 00:44:59,136
Yes, he was.

756
00:45:00,523 --> 00:45:04,002
- He was leading the witness, my Lord.
- Ah.

757
00:45:04,003 --> 00:45:07,242
Well, I think you ought to know better,
Sir Maurice.

758
00:45:07,243 --> 00:45:08,802
It might almost appear
that you were trying

759
00:45:08,803 --> 00:45:10,962
to take advantage
of counsel's lack of experience.

760
00:45:10,963 --> 00:45:13,403
I had no such intention.

761
00:45:13,483 --> 00:45:16,002
I believe I am experienced enough
to know that

762
00:45:16,003 --> 00:45:19,442
I couldn't pull the wool over your eyes,
my Lord.

763
00:45:19,443 --> 00:45:23,162
I have no further questions.
Thank you, Sergeant.

764
00:45:23,163 --> 00:45:24,483
Miss Deed.

765
00:45:30,643 --> 00:45:33,522
You want to cross-examine the witness?

766
00:45:33,523 --> 00:45:36,963
No. I mean,

767
00:45:38,163 --> 00:45:41,242
could we have a short adjournment
until my leader returns, my Lord?

768
00:45:41,243 --> 00:45:43,153
(LAUGHING)

769
00:45:43,283 --> 00:45:47,042
well, as much as I think we would all
appreciate the rest of the day off,

770
00:45:47,043 --> 00:45:51,322
I don't think we can be quite
so cavalier with the Court's time.

771
00:45:51,323 --> 00:45:53,362
You are across all of
the arguments, are you?

772
00:45:53,363 --> 00:45:55,636
Yes, I think so, my Lord.

773
00:45:56,123 --> 00:45:58,722
'Cause I think you should be.

774
00:45:58,723 --> 00:46:00,802
I am prepared to give you
a 10-minute adjournment

775
00:46:00,803 --> 00:46:06,258
so you can consult your leader
or your instructing solicitor.

776
00:46:09,323 --> 00:46:13,762
Jo, this is Charlie. Sir Maurice
has finished his examination early,

777
00:46:13,763 --> 00:46:15,913
what do I do?

778
00:46:16,003 --> 00:46:17,602
Call me as soon as you can.

779
00:46:17,603 --> 00:46:19,642
Charlie, you don't have to cross-examine
this witness.

780
00:46:19,643 --> 00:46:24,803
My dad... The judge, expects me to.
I'll talk to him.

781
00:46:33,843 --> 00:46:37,207
Judge? Charlie would like to you see.

782
00:46:37,403 --> 00:46:39,043
Charlie who?

783
00:46:39,123 --> 00:46:43,282
Well, do we know another Charlie,
apart from your daughter?

784
00:46:43,283 --> 00:46:46,842
- Is Sir Maurice with her?
- I don't think so.

785
00:46:46,843 --> 00:46:49,682
Well, then I can't see her.
You know the rules as well as I do.

786
00:46:49,683 --> 00:46:53,042
- You do break them occasionally.
- well, not for my daughter.

787
00:46:53,043 --> 00:46:55,316
Not when she's before me.

788
00:47:02,563 --> 00:47:05,202
Charlie, he won't see you
unless Sir Maurice is there, too.

789
00:47:05,203 --> 00:47:08,442
This is stupid. I can't ask his advice
in front of the prosecution.

790
00:47:08,443 --> 00:47:09,642
Yeah, but it's the rules, Charlie.

791
00:47:09,643 --> 00:47:13,922
Since when has he ever taken notice
of those? He's my dad. I will see him.

792
00:47:13,923 --> 00:47:15,203
Charlie.

793
00:47:18,123 --> 00:47:21,322
- Why can't you see me? This is stupid.
- Get real, Charlie.

794
00:47:21,323 --> 00:47:23,962
You are appearing before me
in the grown-up world of Crown Court.

795
00:47:23,963 --> 00:47:27,082
You have to understand
that you can't see me on your own.

796
00:47:27,083 --> 00:47:29,202
Our relationship
in these circumstances is changed.

797
00:47:29,203 --> 00:47:30,922
I just wanted to ask you one thing.

798
00:47:30,923 --> 00:47:33,002
Well, you can't,
unless you want to risk a retrial.

799
00:47:33,003 --> 00:47:37,242
- Well, you see Jo alone all the time.
- Charlie, this is not up for debate.

800
00:47:37,243 --> 00:47:42,880
Now you have to change now you're
in this world. Go on, hop it.

801
00:47:49,883 --> 00:47:51,722
What do I do, Granddad?

802
00:47:51,723 --> 00:47:54,642
Well, remember he's not your father
when you're appearing before him.

803
00:47:54,643 --> 00:47:57,122
He could have just told me anyway.

804
00:47:57,123 --> 00:47:59,362
Yes, well, I wish I was there,
my darling.

805
00:47:59,363 --> 00:48:04,562
I may not have Solomonic wisdom,
but I'm a very good oracle on evidence.

806
00:48:04,563 --> 00:48:08,562
If I could only be sitting
within whispering distance.

807
00:48:08,563 --> 00:48:11,562
You could always report sick, Charlie.

808
00:48:11,563 --> 00:48:13,722
He'd demand a medical certificate.

809
00:48:13,723 --> 00:48:16,722
Yes. what tack is Mrs Mills taking?

810
00:48:16,723 --> 00:48:19,202
To attack the credibility of the police.

811
00:48:19,203 --> 00:48:23,833
Yes, I suppose she would.
Look, play for time.

812
00:48:24,323 --> 00:48:26,482
Take the policeman through his every

813
00:48:26,483 --> 00:48:28,642
action since he joined the police force.

814
00:48:28,643 --> 00:48:31,722
That'll teach your father
not to grant a proper adjournment.

815
00:48:31,723 --> 00:48:33,043
(LAUGHING)

816
00:48:33,123 --> 00:48:34,669
Thanks, Granddad.

817
00:48:49,443 --> 00:48:53,242
I still can't believe they knocked me
back on my appeal.

818
00:48:53,243 --> 00:48:54,602
I thought I'd got it for sure,

819
00:48:54,603 --> 00:48:57,362
what with Romano
being done for corruption.

820
00:48:57,363 --> 00:48:59,563
So did I and John Deed.

821
00:48:59,643 --> 00:49:02,682
Yeah, well, it's all right for him,
he's not stuck in here.

822
00:49:02,683 --> 00:49:04,762
The irony is
if you would have pleaded guilty,

823
00:49:04,763 --> 00:49:06,802
you would have been paroled
long before now.

824
00:49:06,803 --> 00:49:10,622
I'll never do that.
I'll die in here first.

825
00:49:11,483 --> 00:49:17,029
I won't give up on you, Denny.
I'll find a way, I promise you.

826
00:49:17,363 --> 00:49:18,482
I think there's something in the case

827
00:49:18,483 --> 00:49:19,962
I'm currently working on that could
help you.

828
00:49:19,963 --> 00:49:22,602
The same two detectives were involved.

829
00:49:22,603 --> 00:49:28,802
Why did you send that girl down here?
why did you think I'd talk to her?

830
00:49:28,803 --> 00:49:32,985
I can't be in two places at once.
I'm in court.

831
00:49:33,163 --> 00:49:35,522
I need someone to corroborate
what Terry Rogers is saying

832
00:49:35,523 --> 00:49:38,682
about those detectives
putting a plastic bag over his head.

833
00:49:38,683 --> 00:49:41,322
It's what they did to me.
On the say so of an informant.

834
00:49:41,323 --> 00:49:43,596
Do you know who that was?

835
00:49:44,123 --> 00:49:49,578
There was no way I could get his name.
He was well protected.

836
00:49:50,203 --> 00:49:53,802
What about the policeman who gave you
the information on the detectives?

837
00:49:53,803 --> 00:49:56,802
Could he help with a name,
or corroboration?

838
00:49:56,803 --> 00:49:59,042
He wouldn't step out of the shadows
for me. why would he for you?

839
00:49:59,043 --> 00:50:02,162
Give me his name, let me talk to him.

840
00:50:02,163 --> 00:50:06,713
He won't do it. He's about to retire.
He's scared.

841
00:50:06,803 --> 00:50:09,402
He was on the squad with
the other detectives, wasn't he?

842
00:50:09,403 --> 00:50:11,562
He asked to go out
because of what they were doing.

843
00:50:11,563 --> 00:50:13,602
Denny, this could help your case.

844
00:50:13,603 --> 00:50:18,282
Yeah, but if he clams up,
I'll never get anything out of him.

845
00:50:18,283 --> 00:50:22,556
You've got to trust me with this.
Just trust me.

846
00:50:26,843 --> 00:50:31,662
And for what precisely did you make
your first arrest?

847
00:50:35,043 --> 00:50:39,722
- Widespread cheque-card fraud.
- How much money was involved?

848
00:50:39,723 --> 00:50:43,202
Miss Deed, do you think
we should ask the jury

849
00:50:43,203 --> 00:50:46,122
if they'd like some more coffee
to keep them awake?

850
00:50:46,123 --> 00:50:49,802
- This is all relevant, my Lord.
- Oh, yeah, I'm sure it is.

851
00:50:49,803 --> 00:50:52,894
I just wish you'd go a bit faster.

852
00:50:59,163 --> 00:51:00,803
Sergeant Hill?

853
00:51:02,043 --> 00:51:04,442
- I'm Jo Mills.
- Think I couldn't guess?

854
00:51:04,443 --> 00:51:07,442
I told you on the phone, I don't know
Denny McDowell or Terry Rogers

855
00:51:07,443 --> 00:51:08,882
other than what I've read
in the newspapers.

856
00:51:08,883 --> 00:51:11,402
I never referred to him as Denny.
I'm sure the papers never did.

857
00:51:11,403 --> 00:51:14,202
Denny, Dennis, what's it matter?
I can't help you.

858
00:51:14,203 --> 00:51:16,002
I'm just going on duty.

859
00:51:16,003 --> 00:51:17,522
You're obviously a man with a conscience

860
00:51:17,523 --> 00:51:20,002
or you wouldn't have contacted
Denny McDowell in the first place.

861
00:51:20,003 --> 00:51:22,282
How many more innocent men
are languishing in jail,

862
00:51:22,283 --> 00:51:25,738
men you might be able to help get out?

863
00:51:28,163 --> 00:51:30,800
In case you change your mind.

864
00:51:33,403 --> 00:51:36,494
That's very interesting, Sergeant.

865
00:51:38,123 --> 00:51:39,402
What was the first thing that you did

866
00:51:39,403 --> 00:51:43,202
when you got to the squad office
each morning?

867
00:51:43,203 --> 00:51:47,113
I'd get a cup of coffee,
then check my post.

868
00:51:55,203 --> 00:51:58,442
When did you first use a plastic bag
placed over a suspect's head

869
00:51:58,443 --> 00:52:00,322
in order to get a confession?

870
00:52:00,323 --> 00:52:03,922
- What?
- My Lord, this outrageous assertion

871
00:52:03,923 --> 00:52:09,602
raises it's ugly head once again.
I wager with nothing to substantiate it.

872
00:52:09,603 --> 00:52:14,240
Have you got anything
to substantiate it, Miss Deed?

873
00:52:18,043 --> 00:52:21,082
- Yes.
- Perhaps my learned friend has something

874
00:52:21,083 --> 00:52:24,265
she wishes to share with the Court.

875
00:52:25,883 --> 00:52:30,065
- Do you, Miss Deed?
- In the fullness of time.

876
00:52:30,163 --> 00:52:35,202
- Well, Sergeant?
- No, I've never strong-armed suspects.

877
00:52:35,203 --> 00:52:39,162
Not even when my team arrested
a child murderer.

878
00:52:39,163 --> 00:52:41,482
You mean a suspected child murderer,
surely?

879
00:52:41,483 --> 00:52:45,362
It's up to the Court to decide
whether your suspect is in fact guilty.

880
00:52:45,363 --> 00:52:48,273
Yes, miss. But he was convicted.

881
00:52:49,003 --> 00:52:52,402
So you've never once used force
to get a prisoner to confess?

882
00:52:52,403 --> 00:52:54,473
No. Never.

883
00:52:54,563 --> 00:52:56,202
So you're saying
that you have never used

884
00:52:56,203 --> 00:52:58,842
a plastic bad to get a suspect
to confess?

885
00:52:58,843 --> 00:53:04,934
- No, I haven't. On my baby's eyesight.
- A simple no would suffice.

886
00:53:06,683 --> 00:53:11,442
Well, that woke everybody up, Mrs Mills,
just as it's time to go home.

887
00:53:11,443 --> 00:53:12,898
USHER: All rise.

888
00:53:20,963 --> 00:53:22,042
(MOBILE RINGING)

889
00:53:22,043 --> 00:53:23,273
Hang on.

890
00:53:24,603 --> 00:53:31,422
Jo Mills. I understand your hesitation,
Sergeant. where do you want to meet?

891
00:53:32,363 --> 00:53:33,593
Half an hour?

892
00:53:39,523 --> 00:53:44,796
Troy Romano and Matthew Stone
were by-words for corruption.

893
00:53:46,123 --> 00:53:49,922
I was so proud when I was selected
to the squad.

894
00:53:49,923 --> 00:53:53,196
Then I discovered what was going on.

895
00:53:53,203 --> 00:53:55,113
It turned my stomach.

896
00:53:56,683 --> 00:53:58,683
I'm as bad, I suppose.

897
00:53:59,603 --> 00:54:01,282
Instead of getting out,
I should have stayed

898
00:54:01,283 --> 00:54:03,283
and blown the whistle.

899
00:54:03,603 --> 00:54:07,722
All these years, I've lived with it,
marking time.

900
00:54:07,723 --> 00:54:10,451
Waiting to collect me pension.

901
00:54:11,283 --> 00:54:12,603
It's shameful.

902
00:54:12,683 --> 00:54:18,138
It takes a lot of courage
to step forward and be singled out.

903
00:54:20,323 --> 00:54:24,602
After they nicked Troy Romano,
Matt Stone got out.

904
00:54:24,603 --> 00:54:28,442
I think Superintendent Craddock
helped him pull clear.

905
00:54:28,443 --> 00:54:30,722
Do you know that or are you speculating?

906
00:54:30,723 --> 00:54:34,202
Look, I want to keep my pension,
Mrs Mills.

907
00:54:34,203 --> 00:54:36,642
Now, Dave Craddock was a strange man
in those days.

908
00:54:36,643 --> 00:54:40,280
I think he was a decent bloke, but weak.

909
00:54:41,803 --> 00:54:45,202
It was probably him
who helped Rogers escape.

910
00:54:45,203 --> 00:54:46,482
Are you serious?

911
00:54:46,483 --> 00:54:52,393
You just can't walk out of
police stations once you're in custody.

912
00:54:52,683 --> 00:54:56,882
Romano and Stone would help out robbers
they should have nicked.

913
00:54:56,883 --> 00:54:59,282
To do that, they'd find a body.

914
00:54:59,283 --> 00:55:01,442
Some worthless criminal
they'd put in the frame.

915
00:55:01,443 --> 00:55:03,682
Terry Rogers was the body.

916
00:55:03,683 --> 00:55:07,282
Then they'd get a professional informant
they had to grass him.

917
00:55:07,283 --> 00:55:09,682
Yes, who was that informant,
what was his name?

918
00:55:09,683 --> 00:55:13,882
- I can't tell you.
- You don't know, or you won't tell me?

919
00:55:13,883 --> 00:55:15,323
I don't know.

920
00:55:15,883 --> 00:55:17,522
Even if I did,
I couldn't give it to you.

921
00:55:17,523 --> 00:55:20,562
He's covered
by public interest immunity.

922
00:55:20,563 --> 00:55:24,082
Was it the same informant
that named Denny McDowell?

923
00:55:24,083 --> 00:55:25,993
I think it was, yeah.

924
00:55:26,363 --> 00:55:30,802
How many cases has this informant
been involved in?

925
00:55:30,803 --> 00:55:32,802
I know of eight convictions.

926
00:55:32,803 --> 00:55:36,713
One, Laurence Ray, got overturned
on appeal.

927
00:55:38,043 --> 00:55:41,602
Did they ever put any robbers away
on real evidence?

928
00:55:41,603 --> 00:55:43,482
There were some really dangerous robbers

929
00:55:43,483 --> 00:55:46,722
put away as a result
of some real coppering.

930
00:55:46,723 --> 00:55:52,282
But there was that much pressure on us,
no one much minded how we did our job.

931
00:55:52,283 --> 00:55:56,762
Well, if I don't get the name of the
informant, we're no further forward.

932
00:55:56,763 --> 00:55:59,323
I'm sorry. I've gotta go.

933
00:56:04,603 --> 00:56:08,442
Do you think Superintendent Craddock
really helped Terry Rogers escape?

934
00:56:08,443 --> 00:56:11,171
If he did, I want to know why.

935
00:56:16,723 --> 00:56:18,962
Of course he didn't, Mrs Mills.
why would he let me go?

936
00:56:18,963 --> 00:56:20,042
That's what I'm asking you.

937
00:56:20,043 --> 00:56:24,225
He didn't. I just saw my opportunity
and I ran.

938
00:56:28,643 --> 00:56:30,842
Let's go back, if we may,

939
00:56:30,843 --> 00:56:35,242
to your involvement with
ex-Detective Sergeants Romano and Stone.

940
00:56:35,243 --> 00:56:38,002
They were by-words for corruption
on the squad, were they not?

941
00:56:38,003 --> 00:56:40,562
- I haven't heard that.
- Oh, really?

942
00:56:40,563 --> 00:56:42,722
Even though Romano was sent to prison

943
00:56:42,723 --> 00:56:44,682
and Stone squeaked out

944
00:56:44,683 --> 00:56:47,962
with the help
of Superintendent Craddock?

945
00:56:47,963 --> 00:56:51,562
Tell us the last case you were on
with Romano and Stone.

946
00:56:51,563 --> 00:56:56,322
- I can't remember.
- well, let me refresh your memory.

947
00:56:56,323 --> 00:56:59,882
Wasn't it the conviction of Laurence Ray
for robbery?

948
00:56:59,883 --> 00:57:01,243
I think so.

949
00:57:02,403 --> 00:57:06,362
Did you use a professional informant
to help convict Laurence Ray?

950
00:57:06,363 --> 00:57:07,962
I really don't remember.

951
00:57:07,963 --> 00:57:10,762
Then I suggest you'd better try harder,
Sergeant.

952
00:57:10,763 --> 00:57:13,522
My Lord, your intervention
is hardly fair to this witness.

953
00:57:13,523 --> 00:57:17,602
He is having to recall events
that occurred some 16 or more years ago.

954
00:57:17,603 --> 00:57:19,922
Yes, and I'm reminding him of his duty.

955
00:57:19,923 --> 00:57:24,042
You are forcing him to give answers
that may not be direct memory.

956
00:57:24,043 --> 00:57:27,407
No, no, no. Sit down. well, Sergeant?

957
00:57:28,963 --> 00:57:31,922
My Lord, I think we may be referring
to the informant

958
00:57:31,923 --> 00:57:34,602
who is covered
by the public interest immunity.

959
00:57:34,603 --> 00:57:38,643
- Are we?
- Yes, my Lord.

960
00:57:38,723 --> 00:57:40,602
Then you can't have the information,
Mrs Mills.

961
00:57:40,603 --> 00:57:42,602
This is wholly unfair, my Lord.

962
00:57:42,603 --> 00:57:44,785
Yes, so you keep saying.

963
00:57:44,803 --> 00:57:46,003
(SIGHS)

964
00:57:50,283 --> 00:57:54,362
Can you tell the Court what happened
subsequent to Mr Ray's conviction?

965
00:57:54,363 --> 00:57:56,322
- I don't know.
- Oh, really?

966
00:57:56,323 --> 00:57:58,122
Not that his conviction was found
to be unsafe

967
00:57:58,123 --> 00:58:02,282
and he was freed by the Court of Appeal
after serving nine years?

968
00:58:02,283 --> 00:58:04,642
What happened to your secret supergrass?

969
00:58:04,643 --> 00:58:08,371
- My Lord...
- Mrs Mills, just be careful.

970
00:58:08,723 --> 00:58:10,996
- I don't know.
- Come on.

971
00:58:11,243 --> 00:58:13,722
Did he get a knighthood?
Retire to the Bahamas?

972
00:58:13,723 --> 00:58:14,882
He wasn't my informant.

973
00:58:14,883 --> 00:58:17,442
But one you had plenty of contact with,
did you not?

974
00:58:17,443 --> 00:58:19,722
My Lord, if you allow the defence
to persist

975
00:58:19,723 --> 00:58:21,402
with this line of questioning,

976
00:58:21,403 --> 00:58:24,522
this person might accidentally
be identified in open court.

977
00:58:24,523 --> 00:58:27,362
I'll see you both in chambers now.

978
00:58:27,363 --> 00:58:28,818
USHER: All rise.

979
00:58:32,403 --> 00:58:34,522
What's going on here, Maurice?

980
00:58:34,523 --> 00:58:36,402
Is there evidence to convict this man

981
00:58:36,403 --> 00:58:38,602
without revealing
the informant's identity?

982
00:58:38,603 --> 00:58:41,922
The CPS thinks there is
and I am of that opinion as well.

983
00:58:41,923 --> 00:58:44,122
Rogers did sign a confession.

984
00:58:44,123 --> 00:58:45,522
He didn't write it though.

985
00:58:45,523 --> 00:58:47,682
The informant simply put
his name in the frame.

986
00:58:47,683 --> 00:58:50,442
That is partly how the police function.

987
00:58:50,443 --> 00:58:53,242
Without informants,
there would be no crime watch.

988
00:58:53,243 --> 00:58:55,962
Come on, Maurice.
This is no longer the '80s.

989
00:58:55,963 --> 00:58:58,962
We take a dim view
of professional informants nowadays.

990
00:58:58,963 --> 00:59:01,402
That makes his information
no less valid, my Lord.

991
00:59:01,403 --> 00:59:06,362
Any information that can't be tested
in court is less than reliable.

992
00:59:06,363 --> 00:59:08,882
- Can you proceed?
- I don't think I can guarantee

993
00:59:08,883 --> 00:59:11,642
my client justice
if I cannot examine the informant.

994
00:59:11,643 --> 00:59:14,442
Bring me evidence of that Jo
and I'll reconsider my decision.

995
00:59:14,443 --> 00:59:16,882
- Judge...
- No, the burden is still on the defence

996
00:59:16,883 --> 00:59:18,802
to show that her client can only get
a fair trial

997
00:59:18,803 --> 00:59:20,922
if this person's name is revealed.

998
00:59:20,923 --> 00:59:27,105
We have to keep this under constant
review, Maurice, and you know it.

999
00:59:27,843 --> 00:59:32,402
I don't know how you're going to prove
that, Mrs Mills.

1000
00:59:32,403 --> 00:59:36,002
Have any of the other convictions
that the informant was responsible for

1001
00:59:36,003 --> 00:59:38,362
been overturned? Besides Laurence Ray's?

1002
00:59:38,363 --> 00:59:39,522
That would do it.

1003
00:59:39,523 --> 00:59:42,362
It would show that the informant is
not reliable and therefore

1004
00:59:42,363 --> 00:59:44,722
the public interest has changed.

1005
00:59:44,723 --> 00:59:46,522
There's the one that got Romano nicked.

1006
00:59:46,523 --> 00:59:47,843
How is that?

1007
00:59:47,923 --> 00:59:49,442
Sonny McQueen was the police plant.

1008
00:59:49,443 --> 00:59:51,722
He was approaching Romano
to do corrupt deals.

1009
00:59:51,723 --> 00:59:54,882
Romano double-crossed him on the deal
and fitted him up for robbery.

1010
00:59:54,883 --> 00:59:57,338
Using the informant? Great.

1011
01:00:08,603 --> 01:00:10,363
(BELL RINGING)

1012
01:00:11,683 --> 01:00:13,363
what do you think?

1013
01:00:24,403 --> 01:00:26,767
What do you think of this?

1014
01:00:27,283 --> 01:00:28,920
It's Harry Potter.

1015
01:00:29,843 --> 01:00:31,842
You liked the Harry Potter books,
didn't you?

1016
01:00:31,843 --> 01:00:33,207
They were okay.

1017
01:00:37,923 --> 01:00:41,082
Michael, I'm really sorry
I couldn't come when I said I would.

1018
01:00:41,083 --> 01:00:42,902
I was terribly busy.

1019
01:00:47,243 --> 01:00:52,322
I've asked the social worker
if you could come to my home soon.

1020
01:00:52,323 --> 01:00:53,963
If you'd like to.

1021
01:00:56,803 --> 01:00:59,803
I'm sure it'd be nicer than here.

1022
01:01:00,563 --> 01:01:03,018
Gonna give him all of them?

1023
01:01:04,403 --> 01:01:07,882
Well, I expect you can play
with them, too. what is your name?

1024
01:01:07,883 --> 01:01:10,082
I think it's Hero, I think.

1025
01:01:10,083 --> 01:01:14,720
Well, that's interesting.
He was a Greek, wasn't he?

1026
01:01:18,203 --> 01:01:23,082
It was a girl. She was in love
with Leander who drowned.

1027
01:01:23,083 --> 01:01:25,962
- She committed suicide.
- How do you know that?

1028
01:01:25,963 --> 01:01:29,145
I've got a book on Greek mythology.

1029
01:01:30,643 --> 01:01:34,842
Perhaps that's why he doesn't like
to remember his name.

1030
01:01:34,843 --> 01:01:38,662
Do you want to go and see
The Wizard of Oz?

1031
01:01:44,883 --> 01:01:49,429
I'll pack these things up.
You can have them later.

1032
01:02:03,243 --> 01:02:05,162
DEED: Having looked at the papers

1033
01:02:05,163 --> 01:02:08,682
in the case against
ex-Detectives Romano and Stone...

1034
01:02:08,683 --> 01:02:10,083
Mrs Mills?

1035
01:02:12,603 --> 01:02:14,058
(wHISPERING) Jo?

1036
01:02:16,443 --> 01:02:18,962
Having looked at papers,
I am going to order that

1037
01:02:18,963 --> 01:02:22,042
the Crown discloses the name
of the informant

1038
01:02:22,043 --> 01:02:24,682
previously protected
under public interest immunity.

1039
01:02:24,683 --> 01:02:28,122
My Lord, I would urge you
most strongly not to do that.

1040
01:02:28,123 --> 01:02:29,722
The man's life may be at stake.

1041
01:02:29,723 --> 01:02:33,602
As the informant seems to have conspired
with at least one corrupt detective,

1042
01:02:33,603 --> 01:02:35,202
it can no longer be
in the public interest

1043
01:02:35,203 --> 01:02:37,522
to keep his name secret, can it?

1044
01:02:37,523 --> 01:02:39,563
What is his name?

1045
01:02:39,643 --> 01:02:44,916
I don't have that information,
only his pseudonym, The Cat.

1046
01:02:45,483 --> 01:02:49,042
Then are either of the two detectives
at court, Mrs Mills?

1047
01:02:49,043 --> 01:02:54,082
- No, neither answered their summons.
- All right, then I'll issue a summons.

1048
01:02:54,083 --> 01:02:57,522
Mr Clerk, I want them both brought here
so I can question them on this matter.

1049
01:02:57,523 --> 01:02:59,922
In the meantime,
is Superintendent Craddock here?

1050
01:02:59,923 --> 01:03:05,287
I want to talk to him as well.
And let's have the jury back.

1051
01:03:07,483 --> 01:03:09,042
Superintendent Craddock,

1052
01:03:09,043 --> 01:03:12,862
I'd like to ask you
some questions, please.

1053
01:03:14,243 --> 01:03:17,202
I would like my objection
to this procedure noted.

1054
01:03:17,203 --> 01:03:18,483
It's noted.

1055
01:03:20,323 --> 01:03:23,402
Superintendent Craddock,
a specific allegation

1056
01:03:23,403 --> 01:03:26,922
has been laid against officers
of the crime squad

1057
01:03:26,923 --> 01:03:30,522
to do with actions whilst questioning
the defendant.

1058
01:03:30,523 --> 01:03:34,802
It's alleged that a plastic bag
was placed over his head

1059
01:03:34,803 --> 01:03:36,923
to obtain a confession.

1060
01:03:37,523 --> 01:03:38,722
Did you take part in that?

1061
01:03:38,723 --> 01:03:42,633
- No, my Lord...
- Do sit down, Sir Maurice.

1062
01:03:43,403 --> 01:03:45,562
No, sir.
Police officers don't behave like that.

1063
01:03:45,563 --> 01:03:49,962
Are you saying officers on the squad
never did that?

1064
01:03:49,963 --> 01:03:52,282
Not to my knowledge, sir. No.

1065
01:03:52,283 --> 01:03:54,402
- You're sure?
- Yes, sir.

1066
01:03:54,403 --> 01:03:56,042
Is it your evidence then

1067
01:03:56,043 --> 01:03:58,562
that Terry Rogers willingly confessed
to this crime?

1068
01:03:58,563 --> 01:04:03,482
- My Lord, he signed a statement.
- That's not what I asked him.

1069
01:04:03,483 --> 01:04:06,922
- Superintendent?
- He did willingly confess, sir.

1070
01:04:06,923 --> 01:04:12,722
Then, how did Mr Rogers sustain injuries
to his wrists and chest?

1071
01:04:12,723 --> 01:04:15,322
He had to be cuffed,
he attacked DS Romano.

1072
01:04:15,323 --> 01:04:17,162
It's in the medical report.

1073
01:04:17,163 --> 01:04:22,482
The independent medical report submitted
by the defence suggests

1074
01:04:22,483 --> 01:04:27,033
that his injuries are consistent

1075
01:04:27,123 --> 01:04:31,642
with having had a plastic bag
placed over his head.

1076
01:04:31,643 --> 01:04:33,734
We didn't do that, sir.

1077
01:04:34,643 --> 01:04:38,007
How did you come to arrest Mr Rogers?

1078
01:04:38,083 --> 01:04:40,842
From information supplied to us
by an informant.

1079
01:04:40,843 --> 01:04:42,323
Ah. Now...

1080
01:04:43,763 --> 01:04:48,633
Did the police ever feed
information to the informant?

1081
01:04:49,123 --> 01:04:50,842
Not to my knowledge, no.

1082
01:04:50,843 --> 01:04:54,242
Then we better ask him.
what is the name of this informant?

1083
01:04:54,243 --> 01:04:57,282
I don't know, sir. I didn't run him.

1084
01:04:57,283 --> 01:05:01,322
I see, it might almost appear
to be a conspiracy

1085
01:05:01,323 --> 01:05:03,882
to prevent this informant's name
from coming out.

1086
01:05:03,883 --> 01:05:08,793
But I will have it.
One way or another, I will have it.

1087
01:05:10,243 --> 01:05:14,682
My Lord, we cannot, we must not pursue
this informant in this way.

1088
01:05:14,683 --> 01:05:16,802
To do so is not in the public interest.

1089
01:05:16,803 --> 01:05:19,562
No, Mr James, as Lord Bingham says,

1090
01:05:19,563 --> 01:05:22,202
"we cannot withhold anything under PII

1091
01:05:22,203 --> 01:05:25,042
"if to do so is to deny
a defendant justice."

1092
01:05:25,043 --> 01:05:27,322
The informant could still be at risk
from criminals.

1093
01:05:27,323 --> 01:05:29,362
It's still not worth risking
a man's liberty.

1094
01:05:29,363 --> 01:05:32,122
The man in question may not deserve
his liberty, Sir John.

1095
01:05:32,123 --> 01:05:37,283
That's what I want to test in court.
I will do this.

1096
01:05:37,363 --> 01:05:39,002
If I have to approach
the Attorney General,

1097
01:05:39,003 --> 01:05:41,802
the Home Secretary
and the Lord Chancellor.

1098
01:05:41,803 --> 01:05:44,531
Not necessarily in that order.

1099
01:05:47,643 --> 01:05:52,962
My earnest advice to you, John,
is not to pursue this matter.

1100
01:05:52,963 --> 01:05:55,122
The PII has always prevailed.

1101
01:05:55,123 --> 01:05:57,082
This wretched informant has blighted

1102
01:05:57,083 --> 01:05:59,282
several lives, Joe,
and he should be outed.

1103
01:05:59,283 --> 01:06:01,282
Anyway, it's my decision.

1104
01:06:01,283 --> 01:06:02,962
The powers that be seem to think

1105
01:06:02,963 --> 01:06:07,402
that a lot of his information
is highly sensitive.

1106
01:06:07,403 --> 01:06:09,522
You could put the Home Secretary
on the rack

1107
01:06:09,523 --> 01:06:11,482
and still not squeeze the name
out of him.

1108
01:06:11,483 --> 01:06:14,322
This isn't about protecting
public interest,

1109
01:06:14,323 --> 01:06:20,323
it's about protecting corrupt policemen
and I'm not going to do it.

1110
01:06:20,883 --> 01:06:22,443
Bloody fool.

1111
01:06:26,683 --> 01:06:29,882
It's not fair, Jo,
the judge said we could have his name.

1112
01:06:29,883 --> 01:06:35,242
Maybe we can get it from Romano,
if the summons server can find him.

1113
01:06:35,243 --> 01:06:40,153
What about Sergeant Hill?
Perhaps he knows where he is.

1114
01:06:42,323 --> 01:06:44,522
You must be able to help, Granddad.

1115
01:06:44,523 --> 01:06:49,562
Yeah, but I'm just on my way to see
the Lord Chancellor, to talk, I hope,

1116
01:06:49,563 --> 01:06:53,563
- about my elevation.
- This is important.

1117
01:06:53,643 --> 01:06:56,962
Was the informant's name disclosed
to the trial judge?

1118
01:06:56,963 --> 01:06:59,322
It was Sir Nicholas Jones
in the McDowell trial.

1119
01:06:59,323 --> 01:07:01,322
You know him, can't you ask him?

1120
01:07:01,323 --> 01:07:03,522
Even if I got the man's name
for you, my darling,

1121
01:07:03,523 --> 01:07:06,482
I'm not sure that you, as the junior
on the case, should have it.

1122
01:07:06,483 --> 01:07:08,120
Dad said we could.

1123
01:07:11,123 --> 01:07:13,033
Look, I've got to go.

1124
01:07:22,523 --> 01:07:26,922
- Wow, it looks like my luck's changed.
- Hello, Troy.

1125
01:07:26,923 --> 01:07:27,993
Sam.

1126
01:07:28,963 --> 01:07:32,242
- You want a drink?
- You didn't answer your witness summons.

1127
01:07:32,243 --> 01:07:33,683
I wonder why.

1128
01:07:34,723 --> 01:07:37,905
Here's one straight from the judge.

1129
01:07:39,323 --> 01:07:40,442
You've got to be kidding me.

1130
01:07:40,443 --> 01:07:44,402
If you don't appear forthwith,
you'll find yourself back in jail.

1131
01:07:44,403 --> 01:07:46,522
And you'll find yourself
without your teeth, darling.

1132
01:07:46,523 --> 01:07:50,122
Don't, Troy, or you'll find yourself
in prison forever.

1133
01:07:50,123 --> 01:07:52,487
Her dad's Mr Justice Deed.

1134
01:08:03,723 --> 01:08:05,814
Why are you doing this?

1135
01:08:06,843 --> 01:08:09,571
I'm trying to keep my pension.

1136
01:08:10,603 --> 01:08:11,723
Yeah.

1137
01:08:15,603 --> 01:08:19,240
You're going about it
the wrong way, pal.

1138
01:08:29,163 --> 01:08:32,322
I hope this is a social call, Joe.
And not a rap over the knuckles.

1139
01:08:32,323 --> 01:08:34,882
It's always been in my nature
to keep quiet.

1140
01:08:34,883 --> 01:08:38,242
Hold my opinion.
But I can't do it any longer.

1141
01:08:38,243 --> 01:08:41,243
I shan't. wretched weasels.

1142
01:08:43,083 --> 01:08:47,083
Reptiles. Oleaginous reptiles.

1143
01:08:48,963 --> 01:08:50,402
They passed me over.

1144
01:08:50,403 --> 01:08:55,483
I've just heard that they're
making Oliver Caldicott

1145
01:08:55,563 --> 01:08:58,443
a Law Lord, instead of me.

1146
01:08:58,523 --> 01:09:03,043
Oh, I see. well, I dare say there'll be

1147
01:09:03,123 --> 01:09:06,682
- other opportunities.
- Don't be absurd.

1148
01:09:06,683 --> 01:09:11,229
Caldicott is six years younger
than I am. Reptiles.

1149
01:09:13,363 --> 01:09:14,363
May I?

1150
01:09:14,443 --> 01:09:15,513
Yeah.

1151
01:09:16,523 --> 01:09:18,842
It seems to me, Joe,
that the less you accommodate them,

1152
01:09:18,843 --> 01:09:20,042
the more they respect you.

1153
01:09:20,043 --> 01:09:24,043
- I've been altogether too compliant.
- well.

1154
01:09:24,883 --> 01:09:28,682
You do your best to keep their whole
shoddy system functioning

1155
01:09:28,683 --> 01:09:32,883
and they slap you in the teeth.

1156
01:09:32,963 --> 01:09:36,962
Charlie, your Charlie said as much
to me a year ago.

1157
01:09:36,963 --> 01:09:39,600
I couldn't see it, of course.

1158
01:09:40,043 --> 01:09:42,722
I gather you're still having difficulty

1159
01:09:42,723 --> 01:09:45,402
identifying that wretched informant.

1160
01:09:45,403 --> 01:09:49,602
Yes, there might almost be a conspiracy
to protect him.

1161
01:09:49,603 --> 01:09:56,243
He's deeply implicated
in police corruption.

1162
01:09:58,203 --> 01:10:01,403
His name is Killick. David Killick.

1163
01:10:01,483 --> 01:10:05,033
Joe, how did you get that name?

1164
01:10:06,843 --> 01:10:11,662
I had to buy one of our brethren
several single malts.

1165
01:10:14,683 --> 01:10:17,722
Jo. Jo, is it true,
Dad's outed the informant?

1166
01:10:17,723 --> 01:10:20,122
His name is David Killick,
I've asked the judge to call him.

1167
01:10:20,123 --> 01:10:23,362
I hope you'll accept that
the possible death of this informant

1168
01:10:23,363 --> 01:10:26,727
will be on your conscience, not mine.

1169
01:10:34,163 --> 01:10:35,842
A regular stroke the squad pulled

1170
01:10:35,843 --> 01:10:39,962
was to stick it on someone
on the fringes of crime.

1171
01:10:39,963 --> 01:10:44,082
Often it was someone
who was causing them too much ache.

1172
01:10:44,083 --> 01:10:46,482
They did that all the time.

1173
01:10:46,483 --> 01:10:50,682
That's how Troy Romano came unstuck
when he give me Sonny McQueen to put up.

1174
01:10:50,683 --> 01:10:53,842
Sonny McQueen being the person planted

1175
01:10:53,843 --> 01:10:56,002
to entrap Detective Sergeant Romano?

1176
01:10:56,003 --> 01:10:59,913
Yes, sir. The trap banged tight
shut on him.

1177
01:11:00,123 --> 01:11:02,402
Tell me about Terry Rogers.

1178
01:11:02,403 --> 01:11:05,222
Troy Romano knew him all right.

1179
01:11:06,203 --> 01:11:10,762
Said he was just a life-wasting
smackhead. Not worth snot.

1180
01:11:10,763 --> 01:11:14,962
Always giving everyone ache,
trying to score all the time.

1181
01:11:14,963 --> 01:11:19,122
But, no, the police didn't give me
his details.

1182
01:11:19,123 --> 01:11:22,562
I put him in on information I got about
that robbery independently.

1183
01:11:22,563 --> 01:11:24,362
No, you can't. You can't have.

1184
01:11:24,363 --> 01:11:26,282
USHER: Silence in the court, please.

1185
01:11:26,283 --> 01:11:30,102
You really can't do that.
Sit down, please.

1186
01:11:31,043 --> 01:11:33,042
- DEED: I won't tolerate...
- what now, Jo?

1187
01:11:33,043 --> 01:11:34,482
Well, if you've got a plastic bag,

1188
01:11:34,483 --> 01:11:37,002
I might be able to get Romano
to confess.

1189
01:11:37,003 --> 01:11:39,913
If I don't nail him, we're sunk.

1190
01:11:41,643 --> 01:11:45,722
David Killick gave us Terry Rogers
good as gold.

1191
01:11:45,723 --> 01:11:48,082
Everything else just added up.
That's how we ran the squad.

1192
01:11:48,083 --> 01:11:51,762
As efficient as Swiss time.
They were villains, we put them away.

1193
01:11:51,763 --> 01:11:56,242
Grassers gave us the body,
we made sure the evidence was there.

1194
01:11:56,243 --> 01:11:58,202
Had you had many dealings
with Mr Killick?

1195
01:11:58,203 --> 01:12:00,673
Oh, yeah. All the while.

1196
01:12:01,723 --> 01:12:02,882
At the start, he was a diamond,

1197
01:12:02,883 --> 01:12:06,042
his information helped us hold back
the swill of criminals.

1198
01:12:06,043 --> 01:12:09,771
Then why'd you fire him
as your informant?

1199
01:12:10,843 --> 01:12:14,571
It got so as he was
too keen to please us.

1200
01:12:14,683 --> 01:12:16,682
We noticed he started naming villains

1201
01:12:16,683 --> 01:12:20,320
who he claimed were into stuff big time.

1202
01:12:20,723 --> 01:12:22,996
It just wasn't adding up.

1203
01:12:23,803 --> 01:12:26,962
Did Mr Killick invent Sonny McQueen?

1204
01:12:26,963 --> 01:12:31,042
The man you tried to convict
for a crime you knew he didn't commit,

1205
01:12:31,043 --> 01:12:33,202
one that resulted in you going
to prison.

1206
01:12:33,203 --> 01:12:38,022
I'm ashamed to say that
I did try and put it on Sonny.

1207
01:12:38,443 --> 01:12:40,802
He was an out-and-out villain

1208
01:12:40,803 --> 01:12:43,642
who tried to corrupt me
and other policemen.

1209
01:12:43,643 --> 01:12:48,371
I did overstep the mark
when I tired to put him away.

1210
01:12:49,043 --> 01:12:52,042
I did wrong when I tried to see he got
his just desserts.

1211
01:12:52,043 --> 01:12:55,602
According to the police file,
Sonny McQueen had no criminal record

1212
01:12:55,603 --> 01:12:58,642
and no connection with criminal circles.

1213
01:12:58,643 --> 01:13:00,602
He was brought in by the police

1214
01:13:00,603 --> 01:13:04,042
who were investigating corruption
within your squad.

1215
01:13:04,043 --> 01:13:07,193
No. No, no.

1216
01:13:08,763 --> 01:13:13,642
Sonny's sheet was pulled by corrupt
detectives who were protecting him.

1217
01:13:13,643 --> 01:13:16,242
They wanted to put me away
because I was too efficient.

1218
01:13:16,243 --> 01:13:20,202
Other policemen wanted to stop you
doing your job well?

1219
01:13:20,203 --> 01:13:22,193
Too right, they did.

1220
01:13:22,283 --> 01:13:25,556
I was fitted up for no other reason.

1221
01:13:27,203 --> 01:13:28,763
I'm an honest man

1222
01:13:30,283 --> 01:13:32,842
who made one mistake that ended
his career.

1223
01:13:32,843 --> 01:13:36,722
You ask anyone about my career.
It was exemplary.

1224
01:13:36,723 --> 01:13:39,042
So, all of your other dealings
with criminals

1225
01:13:39,043 --> 01:13:41,362
have been above board, have they,
Mr Romano?

1226
01:13:41,363 --> 01:13:42,636
You may sneer.

1227
01:13:44,563 --> 01:13:46,202
But I was a hard-working policeman

1228
01:13:46,203 --> 01:13:48,482
who put a lot
of dangerous villains away.

1229
01:13:48,483 --> 01:13:53,802
- You'd do anything to stop them.
- You might not like what we did.

1230
01:13:53,803 --> 01:13:57,002
But that's how the likes of you
get to sleep easy.

1231
01:13:57,003 --> 01:14:00,642
By putting plastic bags over the heads
of innocent men?

1232
01:14:00,643 --> 01:14:03,953
Innocent? You pulling my chain?

1233
01:14:04,923 --> 01:14:08,322
So, when did you decide
that the defendant was guilty?

1234
01:14:08,323 --> 01:14:11,596
Was it before or after he confessed?

1235
01:14:12,763 --> 01:14:15,482
- I knew from day one.
- what, you knew he was guilty

1236
01:14:15,483 --> 01:14:19,002
before you arrested him, before you got
him into that interrogation room?

1237
01:14:19,003 --> 01:14:20,683
Of course.

1238
01:14:20,763 --> 01:14:22,642
All I needed was his confession.

1239
01:14:22,643 --> 01:14:24,642
And it didn't matter how you got it.

1240
01:14:24,643 --> 01:14:28,202
You try keeping the streets safe
from scum like him,

1241
01:14:28,203 --> 01:14:31,762
who'd do anything to feed
their vile habits.

1242
01:14:31,763 --> 01:14:34,202
And all the while, you've got
civil liberties' lawyers bleating,

1243
01:14:34,203 --> 01:14:36,593
"Unfair, unfair, unfair."

1244
01:14:36,683 --> 01:14:41,363
You want to save society?
There's a price to pay.

1245
01:14:41,443 --> 01:14:45,322
Only the likes of you,
the nice people with their clean hands,

1246
01:14:45,323 --> 01:14:47,687
they don't like the price.

1247
01:14:47,923 --> 01:14:50,722
You either have an efficient
police force left alone to do its job,

1248
01:14:50,723 --> 01:14:54,843
or a deteriorating,
crime-infested society.

1249
01:14:56,683 --> 01:14:59,282
Do you have any doubt about
that statement, Mr Romano?

1250
01:14:59,283 --> 01:15:00,374
None at all.

1251
01:15:03,443 --> 01:15:05,171
Thank you, my Lord.

1252
01:15:08,363 --> 01:15:15,082
Did David Killick give you information
that led you to arrest Denny McDowell?

1253
01:15:15,083 --> 01:15:16,083
Denny?

1254
01:15:18,083 --> 01:15:19,843
Yeah, he did.

1255
01:15:19,923 --> 01:15:22,962
Was he also someone you needed
to put away at any price

1256
01:15:22,963 --> 01:15:25,762
- to make the streets safe?
- Yeah.

1257
01:15:25,763 --> 01:15:31,036
By putting a plastic bag over his head
to make him confess?

1258
01:15:31,843 --> 01:15:34,042
It was all within the rules, Judge.

1259
01:15:34,043 --> 01:15:38,882
Mr Romano, I'm not sure you're someone
capable of understanding the rules.

1260
01:15:38,883 --> 01:15:40,520
That's what I did.

1261
01:15:43,683 --> 01:15:47,522
I am going to make one last attempt
to get at the truth here.

1262
01:15:47,523 --> 01:15:50,762
I wouldn't lie.
I'm an honest man, Judge.

1263
01:15:50,763 --> 01:15:53,082
Perhaps at some point
you did want to be.

1264
01:15:53,083 --> 01:15:55,082
I can even believe
that deep down inside of you,

1265
01:15:55,083 --> 01:15:57,202
you still have that yearning.

1266
01:15:57,203 --> 01:15:59,682
I can't absolve you for your crimes.

1267
01:15:59,683 --> 01:16:03,162
But I can promise to talk to the
Director of Public Prosecutions

1268
01:16:03,163 --> 01:16:05,722
about offering you immunity
from prosecution...

1269
01:16:05,723 --> 01:16:07,633
- My Lord.
- No, wait.

1270
01:16:08,603 --> 01:16:12,922
Immunity from prosecution in exchange
for the truth now.

1271
01:16:12,923 --> 01:16:17,002
This is the last opportunity
I'm gonna give you.

1272
01:16:17,003 --> 01:16:18,442
I told you the truth.

1273
01:16:18,443 --> 01:16:22,807
Then walk away, Mr Romano,
and take your chances.

1274
01:16:41,443 --> 01:16:42,802
Will I get immunity?

1275
01:16:42,803 --> 01:16:46,440
I shouldn't think it would be a problem.

1276
01:16:47,763 --> 01:16:52,218
Did you put a plastic bag
over Terry Rogers' head?

1277
01:16:55,323 --> 01:16:57,562
It was the only way to get him to talk.

1278
01:16:57,563 --> 01:17:00,123
(ALL MURMURING)

1279
01:17:00,203 --> 01:17:03,840
He's about as worthless as a man can be.

1280
01:17:04,483 --> 01:17:06,562
What does it matter if a junkie
like that goes to prison?

1281
01:17:06,563 --> 01:17:11,482
His very existence is an offence.
Prison is the best place for him.

1282
01:17:11,483 --> 01:17:16,483
Look at him now.
we straightened him out by nicking him.

1283
01:17:18,243 --> 01:17:20,880
Tell me about Denny McDowell.

1284
01:17:21,483 --> 01:17:24,043
Well, he wasn't up to much.

1285
01:17:24,123 --> 01:17:26,851
Why we tolerate such people...

1286
01:17:29,563 --> 01:17:32,282
Denny was a robber
who got away with it too often.

1287
01:17:32,283 --> 01:17:34,282
He deserved to go down.

1288
01:17:34,283 --> 01:17:38,362
So, the main plank of evidence
which Mr Killick provided

1289
01:17:38,363 --> 01:17:40,762
was an invention, wasn't it?

1290
01:17:40,763 --> 01:17:44,322
Back then, we were doing a terrific job.

1291
01:17:44,323 --> 01:17:49,522
Then, they disband us, they put me
inside and now you got anarchy.

1292
01:17:49,523 --> 01:17:51,642
Cameras in the streets don't stop it.

1293
01:17:51,643 --> 01:17:54,842
Criminals mustn't go to prison
for crimes they didn't commit.

1294
01:17:54,843 --> 01:17:57,389
But they were active, Judge.

1295
01:17:57,643 --> 01:18:00,042
We were fighting a war against robbers
in those days.

1296
01:18:00,043 --> 01:18:03,002
Just as we are against terrorists
nowadays.

1297
01:18:03,003 --> 01:18:06,842
And in war, you do what you have to do
to win.

1298
01:18:06,843 --> 01:18:10,002
You get on top of them
before they overwhelm you.

1299
01:18:10,003 --> 01:18:15,002
We have a system in this country
where the police collect the evidence.

1300
01:18:15,003 --> 01:18:19,402
The CPS evaluates it,
the barristers argue that evidence,

1301
01:18:19,403 --> 01:18:22,522
and then the jury determines
what the truth is.

1302
01:18:22,523 --> 01:18:25,922
We would find ourselves in
an increasingly dangerous situation

1303
01:18:25,923 --> 01:18:29,242
if we put all of those decisions
into the hands of one authority,

1304
01:18:29,243 --> 01:18:32,962
whether it's the police
or the Home Secretary.

1305
01:18:32,963 --> 01:18:37,322
Because that is the root
of dictatorship and totalitarianism.

1306
01:18:37,323 --> 01:18:39,142
And I won't have it.

1307
01:18:40,683 --> 01:18:43,320
I'll see counsel in chambers.

1308
01:18:44,043 --> 01:18:45,498
USHER: All rise.

1309
01:18:57,763 --> 01:19:00,582
Please sit down if you want to.

1310
01:19:00,763 --> 01:19:03,162
I'm going to recommend
to the Attorney General

1311
01:19:03,163 --> 01:19:06,562
that all cases involving Troy Romano,
where there is doubt, of course,

1312
01:19:06,563 --> 01:19:08,002
be thoroughly reassessed.

1313
01:19:08,003 --> 01:19:11,002
Personally, I doubt if any
of the convictions are safe.

1314
01:19:11,003 --> 01:19:13,962
Meanwhile, I'm stopping this trial.

1315
01:19:13,963 --> 01:19:17,282
I'm going to direct the jury
to find Mr Rogers not guilty.

1316
01:19:17,283 --> 01:19:21,562
But the informant acquired incriminating
evidence on Rogers independently.

1317
01:19:21,563 --> 01:19:23,962
His evidence is as tainted
as the police evidence.

1318
01:19:23,963 --> 01:19:26,145
It's not worth the risk.

1319
01:19:28,563 --> 01:19:34,243
Will we be prosecuting
each of the policemen concerned?

1320
01:19:34,323 --> 01:19:36,869
Apart from Troy Romano, yes.

1321
01:19:37,283 --> 01:19:41,362
I hope you will permit me to express
before the jury how much we,

1322
01:19:41,363 --> 01:19:45,727
on behalf of the Crown,
agree with your decision.

1323
01:19:46,523 --> 01:19:48,402
Wouldn't miss it for the world, Maurice.

1324
01:19:48,403 --> 01:19:53,858
Personally, I'd recommend Romano
for psychiatric examination.

1325
01:20:04,203 --> 01:20:05,682
WOMAN 1: Congratulations.
wOMAN 2: well done.

1326
01:20:05,683 --> 01:20:07,682
WOMAN 1: That's wonderful news.

1327
01:20:07,683 --> 01:20:10,282
Well done, mate.
we'll see you in the pub, bye.

1328
01:20:10,283 --> 01:20:12,162
I don't know how I can thank you enough,
Mrs Mills.

1329
01:20:12,163 --> 01:20:13,762
You did, Mr Rogers.

1330
01:20:13,763 --> 01:20:16,036
Hang on one second. Kate.

1331
01:20:16,723 --> 01:20:21,162
- Thank you for staying for that.
- I hoped you'd go to prison.

1332
01:20:21,163 --> 01:20:23,602
Well, I though I would for a moment.

1333
01:20:23,603 --> 01:20:27,331
But you didn't do anything wrong,
did you?

1334
01:20:29,043 --> 01:20:34,882
I left you and your mum. That was
about as wrong as anything I could do.

1335
01:20:34,883 --> 01:20:38,520
It was me who told the police about you.

1336
01:20:39,763 --> 01:20:43,282
Couldn't have been anyone else,
could it?

1337
01:20:43,283 --> 01:20:45,556
Why aren't you mad at me?

1338
01:20:46,123 --> 01:20:48,402
Look, I want to get out of this place.

1339
01:20:48,403 --> 01:20:52,131
Do you want to go
and get a cup of coffee?

1340
01:20:52,803 --> 01:20:53,985
Not now, Dad.

1341
01:21:01,843 --> 01:21:05,934
Sometimes it takes
more than one conversation.

1342
01:21:12,323 --> 01:21:14,402
The Lord Chancellor
and the Attorney General

1343
01:21:14,403 --> 01:21:16,882
are furious about the course
you've embarked upon.

1344
01:21:16,883 --> 01:21:19,442
Are you going to try
and turn me front it, Joe?

1345
01:21:19,443 --> 01:21:21,643
Not for a moment.

1346
01:21:22,243 --> 01:21:25,162
Maybe I am being a bit too critical
of the police.

1347
01:21:25,163 --> 01:21:26,345
Yes, you are.

1348
01:21:27,243 --> 01:21:29,122
I believe that an effective
police service

1349
01:21:29,123 --> 01:21:31,562
requires support and encouragement,

1350
01:21:31,563 --> 01:21:33,654
not constant criticism.

1351
01:21:34,403 --> 01:21:38,222
We're all in need of examples,
not critics.

1352
01:21:38,683 --> 01:21:42,682
There's something else I called about,
the Denny McDowell appeal.

1353
01:21:42,683 --> 01:21:45,774
Some new facts have come to light.

1354
01:21:51,683 --> 01:21:54,562
Mimi, come here, baby. Good dog.

1355
01:21:54,563 --> 01:21:56,082
Did you hear the news?

1356
01:21:56,083 --> 01:21:58,482
The Appeal Court ordered
Denny McDowell's immediate release

1357
01:21:58,483 --> 01:22:00,562
saying that his conviction was unsafe.

1358
01:22:00,563 --> 01:22:05,042
- Calls for a celebration.
- Coffee's good, thanks.

1359
01:22:05,043 --> 01:22:07,602
I've still got work to do tonight.

1360
01:22:07,603 --> 01:22:10,122
Back to TwOCs and shoplifting.

1361
01:22:10,123 --> 01:22:12,602
That's being a barrister, Chaz.

1362
01:22:12,603 --> 01:22:15,282
Don't know if I've got the stamina, Dad.

1363
01:22:15,283 --> 01:22:17,362
I went to bed at 2 a.m.
and then had to schlep out

1364
01:22:17,363 --> 01:22:21,727
to Chelmsford Crown Court
for a bail application.

1365
01:22:23,003 --> 01:22:26,367
Why didn't Jo call me about McDowell?

1366
01:22:27,643 --> 01:22:30,122
I saw her earlier with Michael.

1367
01:22:30,123 --> 01:22:34,082
- I don't think he's very happy.
- Can you wonder, poor lad.

1368
01:22:34,083 --> 01:22:36,322
What's gonna happen
to those corrupt policemen

1369
01:22:36,323 --> 01:22:38,442
- you said should be arrested?
- Nothing,

1370
01:22:38,443 --> 01:22:40,682
unless I issue a warrant
for their arrest.

1371
01:22:40,683 --> 01:22:43,802
Make myself even more unpopular
with the government.

1372
01:22:43,803 --> 01:22:47,349
When have you let that stop you before?

1373
01:22:48,083 --> 01:22:49,083
I'll do it.

1374
01:22:50,203 --> 01:22:51,243
I'll do it.

1375
01:22:56,723 --> 01:23:00,242
Well, I'm not sure it's such
a good idea, Mrs Mills.

1376
01:23:00,243 --> 01:23:03,962
Judge shouldn't really meet
with a former defendant, you know that.

1377
01:23:03,963 --> 01:23:06,418
I can only ask, Mrs Cooper.

1378
01:23:06,923 --> 01:23:08,122
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

1379
01:23:08,123 --> 01:23:09,163
Come in.

1380
01:23:09,243 --> 01:23:12,789
- Mrs Mills and Michael, Judge.
- Hello.

1381
01:23:13,643 --> 01:23:16,202
We are going to see
The Wizard of Oz tonight.

1382
01:23:16,203 --> 01:23:18,962
We wondered if you'd like to join us?

1383
01:23:18,963 --> 01:23:21,322
I'd love to, but I'm afraid...
Thanks, Coop.

1384
01:23:21,323 --> 01:23:23,162
I don't get on very well with musicals.

1385
01:23:23,163 --> 01:23:26,802
I got very scared in one
when I was a kid and I had to leave.

1386
01:23:26,803 --> 01:23:31,167
I think you'd enjoy it.
we could hold your hands.

1387
01:23:32,563 --> 01:23:34,442
There's another reason I'm here, John.

1388
01:23:34,443 --> 01:23:36,602
Terry Rogers wants to ask you a favour.

1389
01:23:36,603 --> 01:23:38,002
I don't think that's a very good idea.

1390
01:23:38,003 --> 01:23:39,458
It is important.

1391
01:23:39,723 --> 01:23:43,602
I can't see a defendant who's acquittal
I've just directed.

1392
01:23:43,603 --> 01:23:47,149
- He's waiting in court.
- Only for you.

1393
01:23:51,163 --> 01:23:56,982
All right, but only with Coop
and the court stenographer present.

1394
01:24:02,843 --> 01:24:03,913
Mr Rogers.

1395
01:24:06,923 --> 01:24:09,682
You have something important
you want to tell me?

1396
01:24:09,683 --> 01:24:10,683
Yes, sir.

1397
01:24:11,603 --> 01:24:15,882
I saw that you issued a warrant
for the arrest of those two policemen

1398
01:24:15,883 --> 01:24:18,323
and the one that retired.

1399
01:24:19,323 --> 01:24:22,602
I want to plead with you
not to go after them.

1400
01:24:22,603 --> 01:24:26,682
You'd have to give me a very good reason
why not.

1401
01:24:26,683 --> 01:24:30,593
Well, I can't give you a reason
in law, sir.

1402
01:24:31,363 --> 01:24:34,682
I know, in a sense, they took away
16 years of my life,

1403
01:24:34,683 --> 01:24:37,802
me having to be on the run
all that time.

1404
01:24:37,803 --> 01:24:40,322
But, in another way,
they gave me back my life.

1405
01:24:40,323 --> 01:24:42,869
That wasn't their intention.

1406
01:24:43,883 --> 01:24:45,883
It may have been, sir.

1407
01:24:46,283 --> 01:24:49,374
Mr Craddock's intention, at least.

1408
01:24:50,123 --> 01:24:53,396
It was him that helped me to escape.

1409
01:24:54,163 --> 01:24:57,618
And what happened as a result was that

1410
01:24:58,123 --> 01:24:59,487
I found myself.

1411
01:25:00,683 --> 01:25:03,722
I'm seeing Kate again, and my ex-wife.

1412
01:25:03,723 --> 01:25:07,162
She said that she's seen something
in me she saw in the beginning.

1413
01:25:07,163 --> 01:25:12,082
They did put a plastic bag over your
head and force you to confess.

1414
01:25:12,083 --> 01:25:13,083
Yeah.

1415
01:25:17,963 --> 01:25:18,963
Dad.

1416
01:25:23,563 --> 01:25:26,442
But not to something that I didn't do.

1417
01:25:26,443 --> 01:25:28,989
I was there on that robbery.

1418
01:25:31,243 --> 01:25:35,442
How I got there, I just don't know.
I was completely off my head.

1419
01:25:35,443 --> 01:25:36,962
I thought I'd gone there to collect
some drugs.

1420
01:25:36,963 --> 01:25:39,145
I know that's no excuse,

1421
01:25:41,843 --> 01:25:44,682
but I'm sorry I said I wasn't guilty.

1422
01:25:44,683 --> 01:25:46,282
Having met Kate again,
I couldn't bear to wait

1423
01:25:46,283 --> 01:25:49,522
another 16 years to get to know her.

1424
01:25:49,523 --> 01:25:53,978
It was she that persuaded me
to come and tell you.

1425
01:25:56,883 --> 01:25:58,522
Now you've been acquitted,
Mr Rogers,

1426
01:25:58,523 --> 01:26:02,722
there's nothing that I or anybody else
can do about your confession.

1427
01:26:02,723 --> 01:26:06,082
That doesn't absolve their behaviour,
though.

1428
01:26:06,083 --> 01:26:08,082
I was a very different person then.

1429
01:26:08,083 --> 01:26:10,882
I'm sure they were very different men
when they

1430
01:26:10,883 --> 01:26:13,974
forced a plastic bag over my head.

1431
01:26:14,643 --> 01:26:16,882
They were under pressure to get results.

1432
01:26:16,883 --> 01:26:21,042
It's what society wanted, after all,
was to stop armed robberies.

1433
01:26:21,043 --> 01:26:24,316
Why did Craddock help you to escape?

1434
01:26:25,803 --> 01:26:27,793
I gave him

1435
01:26:27,883 --> 01:26:30,442
the names of about a dozen detectives

1436
01:26:30,443 --> 01:26:32,642
who were busting drug dealers,
confiscating their gear

1437
01:26:32,643 --> 01:26:36,007
and selling it back to other dealers.

1438
01:26:36,683 --> 01:26:40,320
But I like to think it wasn't just that.

1439
01:26:41,283 --> 01:26:44,362
Maybe he saw something in me that,
up to that point, I hadn't seen myself.

1440
01:26:44,363 --> 01:26:46,248
He must have had it in him
or he wouldn't have

1441
01:26:46,250 --> 01:26:49,842
risked his life to save those two people
like he did.

1442
01:26:49,843 --> 01:26:52,962
Someone gave me a chance
when I was on the run.

1443
01:26:52,963 --> 01:26:55,327
That's all any of us need.

1444
01:26:55,443 --> 01:26:59,080
The right chance
and a bit of compassion.

1445
01:26:59,403 --> 01:27:03,202
Well, there's only one way to put
your theory to the test, Mr Rogers.

1446
01:27:03,203 --> 01:27:05,749
And that's to set them free.

1447
01:27:18,283 --> 01:27:20,282
You'd never get another ticket to
The Wizard of Oz

1448
01:27:20,283 --> 01:27:22,920
at this short notice, anyway.

1449
01:27:25,889 --> 01:27:28,617
Time to face your fears, John.

